(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e2b.3d85018cd5.js"]={"26":[{"id":"sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic α-amylase digests starch into which products?","choices":{"A":"Glucose, galactose, and free fructose units","B":"Maltose, maltotriose, α-limit dextrins","C":"Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose units","D":"Glycogen, amylopectin, and cellulose","E":"Galactose, maltose, and sucrose units"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreatic amylase hydrolyzes interior 1,4-glycosidic bonds in starch and yields three oligosaccharides: α-limit dextrins, maltose, and maltotriose. Brush-border α-dextrinase, maltase, and sucrase then produce glucose. Monosaccharides are the brush-border end products, not amylase products. Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose are dietary disaccharides. Glycogen and amylopectin are substrates, and cellulose is not digested. Glucose, galactose, and fructose are the final monosaccharides made by brush-border enzymes; α-amylase stops at α-limit dextrins, maltose, and maltotriose. Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose are the dietary disaccharides that skip the amylase step, not products of starch digestion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.27). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: salivary amylase starts starch digestion but is inactivated by low gastric pH (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: human amylase does not hydrolyze the β-1,4 bonds of cellulose (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Glucose, galactose, and fructose are the final monosaccharid","C":"Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose are the dietary disaccharide"}},{"id":"sdl26_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Brush-border lactase digests lactose into which monosaccharides?","choices":{"A":"Glucose and fructose","B":"Two glucose molecules","C":"Glucose and galactose","D":"Galactose and fructose","E":"Glucose and maltose"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lactase splits each lactose into one glucose and one galactose. Glucose plus fructose is the sucrase product, and two glucose molecules come from trehalose or maltose. Galactose with fructose is not a dietary disaccharide product, and maltose is a disaccharide. Glucose and fructose are the products of sucrase acting on sucrose, not of lactase. Two glucose molecules come from trehalase acting on trehalose, not from lactase acting on lactose. Galactose comes only from lactose and fructose only from sucrose, so no single brush-border disaccharidase yields galactose plus fructose. Maltose is itself a disaccharide that maltase digests to glucose, so it is not a product of lactose digestion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sucrase splits sucrose into glucose and fructose, and trehalase splits trehalose into two glucose molecules (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: about 80 percent of carbohydrate end product is glucose, with galactose and fructose making up the rest (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Glucose and fructose are the products of sucrase acting on s","B":"Two glucose molecules come from trehalase acting on trehalos","D":"Galactose comes only from lactose and fructose only from suc","E":"Maltose is itself a disaccharide that maltase digests to glu"}},{"id":"sdl26_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Undigested lactose in lactase deficiency causes diarrhea by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Active chloride secretion by crypts","B":"Faster colonic motility from gastrin","C":"Mucosal inflammation and blood loss","D":"Bile acid irritation of colonocytes","E":"Osmotic water retention in the lumen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Nonabsorbable lactose stays in the lumen and holds an equivalent amount of water to keep contents isosmotic, which causes osmotic diarrhea. Crypt chloride secretion drives secretory diarrhea. Gastrin and motility changes are not the mechanism. Inflammation with blood loss suggests colitis. Bile acid diarrhea follows ileal disease. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption, Box 8.2). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic bacteria ferment undigested lactose and produce gas, causing bloating and flatulence (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: lactose intolerance is managed by avoiding milk products or adding lactase (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"Which apical transporter moves glucose and galactose into intestinal epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"GLUT5","B":"GLUT2","C":"GLUT4","D":"SGLT1","E":"NHE3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"SGLT1, the Na⁺-glucose cotransporter, carries glucose and galactose across the apical membrane against an electrochemical gradient. GLUT5 is the apical fructose transporter. GLUT2 moves monosaccharides across the basolateral membrane. GLUT4 is the insulin-responsive carrier of muscle and fat. NHE3 is an apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger. GLUT5 is the apical fructose-specific facilitated-diffusion transporter, not the carrier for glucose and galactose. GLUT2 sits on the basolateral membrane and lets monosaccharides exit into blood by facilitated diffusion; it is not the apical uptake step. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.28). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT1 in intestine and SGLT2 in the proximal tubule both use the sodium gradient for glucose uptake (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: glucose and galactose absorption resembles transport in the early proximal convoluted tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"GLUT5 is the apical fructose-specific facilitated-diffusion ","B":"GLUT2 sits on the basolateral membrane and lets monosacchari"}},{"id":"sdl26_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"What supplies the energy for glucose uptake by SGLT1?","choices":{"A":"Direct ATP hydrolysis by the SGLT1 protein","B":"Sodium gradient set by the Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase","C":"Proton gradient set by apical exchange","D":"Glucose gradient into the enterocyte","E":"Potassium gradient through apical channels"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that the energy for SGLT1 does not come directly from ATP but from the Na⁺ gradient across the apical membrane, which the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase creates and maintains. This is secondary active transport. Proton gradients drive peptide uptake. Glucose moves against its gradient on SGLT1. Potassium gradients do not drive it. SGLT1 does not hydrolyze ATP itself; its energy comes from the Na⁺ gradient that the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase maintains. An H+ gradient set by apical Na+-H+ exchange drives dipeptide and tripeptide uptake, not glucose entry on SGLT1. SGLT1 moves glucose into the cell against its electrochemical gradient, so a glucose gradient cannot supply the energy. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary active transport couples uphill solute movement to downhill Na⁺ movement (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Na⁺-glucose cotransport in the small intestine resembles transport in the early proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"SGLT1 does not hydrolyze ATP itself; its energy comes from t","C":"An H+ gradient set by apical Na+-H+ exchange drives dipeptid","D":"SGLT1 moves glucose into the cell against its electrochemica"}},{"id":"sdl26_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"How does fructose cross the apical membrane of intestinal epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Sodium cotransport via SGLT1","B":"Facilitated diffusion via GLUT4","C":"Proton cotransport via PepT1","D":"Primary active ATP pumping","E":"Facilitated diffusion via GLUT5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fructose crosses the apical membrane by facilitated diffusion on the fructose-specific transporter GLUT5 and leaves across the basolateral membrane on GLUT2. No cotransporter or energy-requiring step is involved, so fructose moves down its gradient. SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose. GLUT4 is insulin regulated in muscle and fat. PepT1 carries small peptides. SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose with Na⁺; fructose uptake involves no cotransporter or energy-requiring step. Fructose absorption uses only facilitated diffusion, so it has no energy-requiring step and cannot move against its gradient. H+-dependent cotransporters carry dipeptides and tripeptides into the enterocyte, whereas fructose enters by facilitated diffusion on GLUT5. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: fructose absorption by facilitated diffusion does not proceed against an electrochemical gradient (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: glucose and galactose exit the basolateral membrane on GLUT2, as fructose does (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose with Na⁺; fructose uptak","D":"Fructose absorption uses only facilitated diffusion, so it h","C":"H+-dependent cotransporters carry dipeptides and tripeptides"}},{"id":"sdl26_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protease begins protein digestion in the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin","B":"Elastase","C":"Renin","D":"Pepsin","E":"Thrombin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Protein digestion begins with pepsin, formed when pepsinogen from chief cells meets low gastric pH. Trypsin and elastase are pancreatic proteases that act in the small intestine. Renin is a renal enzyme, and thrombin is a coagulation protease. Trypsin is a pancreatic protease that works in the small intestine after enterokinase activates trypsinogen, not in the stomach. Elastase is secreted by the pancreas as proelastase and is activated by trypsin in the small intestine, so it does not begin protein digestion in the stomach. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chief cells secrete pepsinogen, and parietal cells secrete H⁺ that activates it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: pepsin is an endopeptidase that digests collagen in meat (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Trypsin is a pancreatic protease that works in the small int","B":"Elastase is secreted by the pancreas as proelastase and is a"}},{"id":"sdl26_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Above what pH is pepsin denatured and inactivated?","choices":{"A":"Above pH 5","B":"Above pH 2","C":"Above pH 3","D":"Above pH 9","E":"Above pH 11"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The three pepsin isozymes have pH optima between 1 and 3, and pepsin is denatured above pH 5, so it stops acting once chyme is neutralized by duodenal bicarbonate. pH 2 and 3 fall in the optimum range. pH 9 and 11 overshoot the textbook threshold. Pepsin isozymes have optimum activity between pH 1 and 3, so pepsin is active at pH 2, not denatured. Pepsin isozymes have optimum activity between pH 1 and 3, so pepsin is still active at pH 3, not denatured. Pepsin is already denatured above pH 5, so pH 9 is far past the point of inactivation. Pepsin is already denatured above pH 5, so pH 11 is far past the point of inactivation. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic HCO₃⁻ neutralizes duodenal chyme and inactivates pepsin (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: pepsin handles about 10 to 20 percent of total protein digestion (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"Pepsin isozymes have optimum activity between pH 1 and 3, so","C":"Pepsin isozymes have optimum activity between pH 1 and 3, so pepsin is s","D":"Pepsin is already denatured above pH 5, so pH 9 is far past ","E":"Pepsin is already denatured above pH 5, so pH 11 is far past"}},{"id":"sdl26_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pancreatic proteases are exopeptidases?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin and chymotrypsin","B":"Pepsin and elastase","C":"Carboxypeptidase A and B","D":"Elastase and trypsin","E":"Enterokinase and pepsin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Exopeptidases hydrolyze one amino acid at a time from the C-terminal end; carboxypeptidases A and B are the pancreatic exopeptidases. Trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and pepsin are endopeptidases that cleave interior bonds. Enterokinase is a brush-border activating enzyme. Trypsin and chymotrypsin are endopeptidases that cut interior peptide bonds, not exopeptidases. Pepsin and elastase are endopeptidases that cut interior peptide bonds, not exopeptidases. Elastase and trypsin are both endopeptidases that hydrolyze interior peptide bonds; only carboxypeptidases A and B remove one amino acid at a time from the C-terminal end. Pepsin is a gastric endopeptidase and enterokinase is the brush-border enzyme that activates trypsinogen, so neither is a pancreatic exopeptidase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: brush-border aminopeptidases and dipeptidases finish oligopeptide digestion to absorbable units (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: elastase digests the elastin fibers that hold meat together (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Trypsin and chymotrypsin are endopeptidases that cut interio","B":"Pepsin and elastase are endopeptidases that cut interior pep","D":"Elastase and trypsin are both endopeptidases that hydrolyze ","E":"Pepsin is a gastric endopeptidase and enterokinase is the br"}},{"id":"sdl26_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme first converts trypsinogen to trypsin?","choices":{"A":"Aminopeptidase","B":"Chymotrypsin","C":"Elastase","D":"Cathepsin B","E":"Enterokinase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brush-border enterokinase converts trypsinogen to trypsin; trypsin then activates the remaining zymogens and more trypsinogen. Aminopeptidase is a brush-border exopeptidase that trims oligopeptides. Chymotrypsin and elastase are themselves products of trypsin activation. Cathepsin B is a lysosomal protease. Chymotrypsin is made from chymotrypsinogen by trypsin, so it acts downstream of trypsin rather than starting the cascade. Brush-border peptidases such as aminopeptidase trim oligopeptides after the pancreatic proteases are already active; the first activating step is enterokinase acting on trypsinogen. Elastase is secreted as inactive proelastase and is itself converted by trypsin, so it lies downstream of trypsin rather than starting the cascade. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins, Fig. 8.29). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enterokinase is also called enteropeptidase (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic secretions). COMLEX: trypsin activates trypsinogen in an autocatalytic step after the first activation (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Chymotrypsin is made from chymotrypsinogen by trypsin, so it","A":"Brush-border peptidases such as aminopeptidase trim oligopep","C":"Elastase is secreted as inactive proelastase and is itself c"}},{"id":"sdl26_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is enterokinase located?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic zymogen granules","B":"Gastric chief cells","C":"Intestinal brush border","D":"Pancreatic ductal cells","E":"Hepatic bile canaliculi"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enterokinase is a brush-border enzyme of the small intestinal epithelium, so trypsinogen is first activated in the intestinal lumen, not in the pancreas. Zymogen granules hold inactive precursors. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen. Ductal cells secrete HCO₃⁻-rich fluid. Bile canaliculi carry bile. Gastric chief cells secrete pepsinogen, the precursor of pepsin, not enterokinase. Zymogen granules store the pancreatic peptidases as inactive precursors inside acinar cells, and trypsinogen is activated only after secretion, by enterokinase on the intestinal brush border. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas; Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: separating zymogen storage from the activating enzyme protects the pancreas from autodigestion (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: trypsin from the first activation step converts further trypsinogen to trypsin (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Gastric chief cells secrete pepsinogen, the precursor of pep","A":"Zymogen granules store the pancreatic peptidases as inactive"}},{"id":"sdl26_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme converts chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, and the procarboxypeptidases to active forms?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin","B":"Enterokinase","C":"Pepsin","D":"Lipase","E":"Amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Once a small amount of trypsin is made by enterokinase, trypsin catalyzes the conversion of the other inactive precursors to chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases A and B. Enterokinase acts on trypsinogen. Pepsin works in the stomach. Lipase and amylase digest fat and starch and do not activate proteases. Enterokinase only starts the cascade by activating trypsinogen; trypsin then converts the other inactive proteases. Pepsin works only at low gastric pH and is denatured above pH 5, so its action ends in the duodenum and it does not activate the pancreatic zymogens. Pancreatic amylase hydrolyzes interior 1,4-glycosidic bonds of starch, not the peptide bonds that convert protease precursors to active enzymes. Pancreatic lipase is secreted as the active enzyme and hydrolyzes triglyceride; it does not activate the protease precursors. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: absence of trypsin alone looks like loss of the pancreatic proteases because trypsin activates each precursor (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: trypsin also activates procolipase and prophospholipase A₂ (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic secretions).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Enterokinase only starts the cascade by activating trypsinog","C":"Pepsin works only at low gastric pH and is denatured above p","E":"Pancreatic amylase hydrolyzes interior 1,4-glycosidic bonds ","D":"Pancreatic lipase is secreted as the active enzyme and hydro"}},{"id":"sdl26_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"Dipeptides and tripeptides cross the apical membrane by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Sodium cotransport","B":"Facilitated diffusion","C":"Primary active transport","D":"Proton cotransport","E":"Receptor endocytosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Separate H⁺-dependent cotransporters carry dipeptides and tripeptides into the cell, using an H⁺ gradient created by the apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger. Sodium cotransport carries free L-amino acids. Facilitated diffusion is the basolateral exit route for amino acids. Primary active transport and endocytosis are not the described routes. Na⁺-dependent cotransporters carry free L-amino acids; dipeptides and tripeptides use separate H⁺-dependent cotransporters. Facilitated diffusion is how amino acids leave across the basolateral membrane into blood, not how dipeptides and tripeptides cross the apical membrane. Peptide uptake is powered by an H+ gradient that the apical Na+-H+ exchanger creates, not by an apical ATPase pumping the peptides directly. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins, Fig. 8.31). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the apical peptide carrier is PepT1 (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: peptide carriers allow protein nutrition despite single amino acid transporter defects (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Na⁺-dependent cotransporters carry free L-amino acids; dipep","B":"Facilitated diffusion is how amino acids leave across the ba","C":"Peptide uptake is powered by an H+ gradient that the apical "}},{"id":"sdl26_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"In which form is most ingested protein absorbed?","choices":{"A":"Free single amino acids","B":"Dipeptides and tripeptides","C":"Intact dietary protein molecules","D":"Large oligopeptides","E":"Long polypeptide chains"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that most ingested protein is absorbed in the dipeptide and tripeptide forms rather than as free amino acids. Intact proteins and larger oligopeptides are not absorbable and must be broken down by brush-border peptidases. Free amino acids are absorbed, but most ingested protein enters the cells as dipeptides and tripeptides rather than as free amino acids. Oligopeptides are not absorbable; brush-border proteases must first cut them into amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides. Intact dietary protein must first be hydrolyzed by pancreatic and brush-border proteases, because only amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides are absorbable. Larger peptides (oligopeptides) are not absorbable and are further hydrolyzed by brush-border proteases into amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proteins are absorbable in larger units than carbohydrates, which must reach monosaccharides (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: oligopeptides are hydrolyzed by brush-border proteases before absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Free amino acids are absorbed, but most ingested protein ent","D":"Oligopeptides are not absorbable; brush-border proteases mus","C":"Intact dietary protein must first be hydrolyzed by pancreati","E":"Larger peptides (oligopeptides) are not absorbable and are f"}},{"id":"sdl26_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to most dipeptides and tripeptides after they enter the epithelial cell?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis by cytoplasmic peptidases","B":"Export back out through apical PepT1","C":"Re-esterification in the smooth ER","D":"Packaging into nascent chylomicrons","E":"Conversion to cellular glycogen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inside the cell, most dipeptides and tripeptides are hydrolyzed to amino acids by cytoplasmic peptidases, and the amino acids exit across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion; a few peptides are absorbed unchanged. PepT1 imports peptides. Re-esterification and chylomicrons belong to lipid absorption. Glycogen storage is unrelated. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal blood receives mostly free amino acids after protein absorption (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: basolateral amino acid exit uses separate carriers for neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has bloating, flatulence, and watery diarrhea within 2 hours of drinking milk. Temperature is 36.9°C, pulse is 78/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. A hydrogen breath test is positive after an oral lactose load. Stool is negative for occult blood. Which stool finding is most likely during symptoms?","choices":{"A":"High stool sodium concentration","B":"Fecal leukocytes and blood","C":"Fat globules on Sudan stain","D":"Diarrhea persisting during fasting","E":"Increased stool osmotic gap"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Undigested lactose holds water osmotically in the lumen, so unmeasured osmoles raise the stool osmotic gap, and the diarrhea stops with fasting. High stool sodium with a low gap fits secretory diarrhea. Leukocytes and blood suggest inflammatory diarrhea. Fat on Sudan stain points to fat malabsorption. Persistent diarrhea while fasting suggests secretion. Undigested lactose causes osmotic diarrhea, whereas fat in stool reflects lipid malabsorption. Lactose-induced diarrhea resolves when lactose is withheld, unlike secretory diarrhea. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Box 8.2 lactose intolerance; Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bacterial fermentation of lactose raises breath hydrogen and lowers stool pH (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: the other brush-border enzymes are preserved in isolated lactase deficiency (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Undigested lactose causes osmotic diarrhea, whereas fat in s","D":"Lactose-induced diarrhea resolves when lactose is withheld, "}},{"id":"sdl26_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-month-old girl has had watery diarrhea with each formula feeding for 3 weeks after an episode of rotavirus gastroenteritis. Temperature is 37.0°C, and pulse is 128/min. She is mildly dehydrated. Stool pH is 5.0 (reference range: 7.0–7.5), and stool reducing substances are positive. Which change in her small intestine best explains this course?","choices":{"A":"Low pancreatic amylase output","B":"Loss of brush-border lactase","C":"Defective apical glucose cotransport","D":"Absent enterokinase activity","E":"Deficient bile salt secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Viral injury to villus tip cells reduces brush-border lactase, so formula lactose reaches the colon undigested and is fermented, lowering stool pH with positive reducing substances; the deficit recovers as villi regrow. Low amylase affects starch, not lactose. A glucose cotransport defect would be congenital. Enterokinase loss impairs protein digestion. Bile salt deficiency causes fat malabsorption. Pancreatic amylase digests starch; lactose is a disaccharide that needs no amylase step. Lactose must first be split by brush-border lactase; glucose cotransport only acts on the monosaccharides produced. Enterokinase activates trypsinogen for protein digestion and has no role in lactose digestion. Bile salts are needed for lipid digestion, not for carbohydrate digestion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (lactase deficiency, Box 8.2); Guyton Ch. 66 (brush-border disaccharidases). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary lactase deficiency follows mucosal injury from gastroenteritis or celiac disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: lactase sits on the villus brush border, where mucosal injury removes it first (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pancreatic amylase digests starch; lactose is a disaccharide","C":"Lactose must first be split by brush-border lactase; glucose","D":"Enterokinase activates trypsinogen for protein digestion and","E":"Bile salts are needed for lipid digestion, not for carbohydr"}},{"id":"sdl26_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes starts acarbose with meals. Two weeks later she reports flatulence and loose stools. Temperature is 36.8°C, and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Hemoglobin A1c is 7.6% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%). Inhibition of which digestive step most directly explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border oligosaccharide hydrolysis","B":"Pancreatic triglyceride ester hydrolysis","C":"Basolateral monosaccharide exit","D":"Apical sodium-glucose cotransport","E":"Gastric pepsin protein hydrolysis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acarbose inhibits brush-border α-glucosidases, so maltose, maltotriose, and α-limit dextrins are not split to glucose and reach the colon, where fermentation causes gas and osmotic stool. Triglyceride hydrolysis is the target of orlistat. Basolateral exit and apical cotransport would act on monosaccharides already formed. Pepsin digests protein. Triglyceride hydrolysis by pancreatic lipase is lipid digestion; oligosaccharides are split by brush-border enzymes. Basolateral GLUT2 exit moves glucose already formed and is not a digestive step. SGLT1 transports glucose only after brush-border enzymes have released it from oligosaccharides. Pepsin digests protein in the stomach, not carbohydrate. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates); First Aid Endocrine (α-glucosidase inhibitors). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: α-glucosidase inhibitors lower postprandial glucose by delaying carbohydrate digestion (First Aid Endocrine, diabetes drugs). COMLEX: oligosaccharides that escape digestion produce osmotic diarrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Triglyceride hydrolysis by pancreatic lipase is lipid digest","C":"Basolateral GLUT2 exit moves glucose already formed and is n","D":"SGLT1 transports glucose only after brush-border enzymes hav","E":"Pepsin digests protein in the stomach, not carbohydrate."}},{"id":"sdl26_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old boy has severe watery diarrhea with breast milk and with standard formula. Temperature is 36.9°C, pulse is 162/min, and weight is below birth weight. Serum urea nitrogen is 28 mg/dL (reference range: 7–18 mg/dL). Diarrhea stops on a formula whose sole carbohydrate is fructose. Which transporter is most likely defective?","choices":{"A":"Apical fructose-specific uniporter","B":"Basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump","C":"Brush-border lactase hydrolase","D":"Apical sodium-glucose cotransporter","E":"Basolateral monosaccharide uniporter"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glucose and galactose share the apical SGLT1 carrier, so a defect causes osmotic diarrhea with any glucose or galactose source, while fructose enters through GLUT5 and is tolerated. Loss of the fructose uniporter would spare glucose. A Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase defect would not be selective. Lactase deficiency would tolerate glucose formula. Loss of the basolateral uniporter would block fructose exit too. Fructose enters on GLUT5, so a GLUT5 defect would spare glucose and galactose rather than fructose. Lactase deficiency affects only lactose; free glucose and galactose would still be absorbed. Basolateral GLUT2 also carries fructose out of the cell, so its loss would impair fructose absorption too. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.28); First Aid Gastrointestinal (malabsorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucose-galactose malabsorption is an autosomal recessive SGLT1 defect (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: fructose is absorbed independently of sodium (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — solute-coupled water absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fructose enters on GLUT5, so a GLUT5 defect would spare gluc","C":"Lactase deficiency affects only lactose; free glucose and ga","E":"Basolateral GLUT2 also carries fructose out of the cell, so "}},{"id":"sdl26_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has profuse watery diarrhea after travel to an area with a cholera outbreak. Temperature is 37.1°C, pulse is 112/min, and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. Serum bicarbonate is 17 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). An oral rehydration solution corrects his volume deficit. Why does this solution work?","choices":{"A":"Glucose blocks crypt chloride secretion","B":"Sodium enters through the apical GLUT5","C":"Glucose stimulates pancreatic fluid outflow","D":"Sodium uptake no longer requires ATP use","E":"Glucose-coupled sodium uptake is preserved"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cholera toxin drives secretion, but apical SGLT1 still couples glucose entry to sodium entry, and water follows the absorbed solute. Glucose does not block chloride secretion. GLUT5 carries fructose, not sodium. Pancreatic fluid adds to losses. Sodium uptake still requires ATP indirectly, because the gradient depends on the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase. GLUT5 is the apical fructose transporter and carries no Na+. The Na+ gradient driving SGLT1 is maintained by the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, so ATP is still used indirectly. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates; oral rehydration in cholera). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: oral rehydration solution uses SGLT1 cotransport of sodium and glucose (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: cholera toxin raises cAMP in crypt cells and opens CFTR chloride channels (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — cholera toxin and secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"GLUT5 is the apical fructose transporter and carries no Na+.","D":"The Na+ gradient driving SGLT1 is maintained by the basolate"}},{"id":"sdl26_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman training for a marathon tolerates more carbohydrate per hour from a gel that mixes glucose and fructose than from one with glucose alone. Pulse is 58/min, and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Fasting glucose is 84 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL). Which property of intestinal absorption best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Fructose activates the glucose cotransporter","B":"Fructose uses a separate apical carrier","C":"Glucose stimulates fructose cotransport","D":"Fructose enters against its gradient","E":"Glucose exits through apical GLUT5"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Glucose uptake is limited by apical SGLT1, while fructose enters through a distinct carrier, GLUT5, by facilitated diffusion, so mixing sugars adds absorptive capacity. Fructose does not activate SGLT1, and it is not cotransported. GLUT5 carries fructose down its gradient. Glucose exits across the basolateral membrane on GLUT2. Fructose absorption involves no cotransporter in the apical membrane. Fructose uses only facilitated diffusion and cannot be absorbed against its gradient. Glucose exits across the basolateral membrane on GLUT2; GLUT5 is the apical fructose carrier. Fructose crosses the apical membrane on GLUT5 by facilitated diffusion with no cotransporter, so it does not act on the glucose cotransporter. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLUT2 carries glucose, galactose, and fructose across the basolateral membrane (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: fructose absorption is passive and depends on its concentration gradient (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — monosaccharide transporters","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Fructose absorption involves no cotransporter in the apical ","D":"Fructose uses only facilitated diffusion and cannot be absor","E":"Glucose exits across the basolateral membrane on GLUT2; GLUT","A":"Fructose crosses the apical membrane on GLUT5 by facilitated"}},{"id":"sdl26_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man is seen 1 year after total gastrectomy for gastric adenocarcinoma. Pulse is 74/min, and blood pressure is 126/78 mm Hg. Serum albumin is 4.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and fecal nitrogen is normal. Which statement best describes his protein digestion?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal proteases complete digestion","B":"Protein digestion falls by about half","C":"Free amino acid uptake becomes defective","D":"Trypsinogen activation fails in duodenum","E":"Undigested protein reaches the colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pepsin starts protein digestion, but pancreatic and brush-border proteases can complete it, so protein digestion and absorption stay normal after gastrectomy or without gastric acid. Halved digestion overstates pepsin's role. Amino acid transport and enterokinase activation are intestinal and intact. Normal fecal nitrogen argues against colonic protein loss. Pepsin is not essential: after gastrectomy, protein digestion and absorption stay normal because pancreatic and brush-border proteases suffice. Amino acid uptake uses intestinal Na+-amino acid cotransporters, and absorption stays normal after gastrectomy. Trypsinogen is activated by brush-border enterokinase, not by gastric acid. Pancreatic and brush-border proteases alone adequately digest protein, so it is not left undigested. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: after total gastrectomy, vitamin B₁₂ deficiency follows loss of intrinsic factor (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: pepsin accounts for a minor share of protein digestion (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — intrinsic factor and vitamin B12","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"Pepsin is not essential: after gastrectomy, protein digestio","C":"Amino acid uptake uses intestinal Na+-amino acid cotransport","D":"Trypsinogen is activated by brush-border enterokinase, not b","E":"Pancreatic and brush-border proteases alone adequately diges"}},{"id":"sdl26_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis from alcohol use has bulky stools and weight loss. Temperature is 36.7°C, and pulse is 88/min. Fecal elastase is 80 µg/g (reference range: >200 µg/g). Fecal nitrogen is increased. Which digestive step is most impaired for dietary protein?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis of lactose to monosaccharides","B":"Hydrolysis of proteins to oligopeptides","C":"Activation of pepsinogen in the stomach","D":"Apical uptake of small peptides","E":"Basolateral exit of amino acids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreatic insufficiency removes trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases, so protein is not broken down to absorbable units and is lost in stool. Lactose hydrolysis is a brush-border step. Pepsinogen activation depends on gastric acid. Peptide uptake and amino acid exit are epithelial transport steps that remain intact. Lactose is hydrolyzed by brush-border lactase, not by pancreatic enzymes. Pepsinogen is activated by low gastric pH, a step independent of the pancreas. Dipeptide and tripeptide uptake uses apical H+-dependent cotransporters of the epithelium, which pancreatic disease spares. Amino acids exit across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion, an epithelial step, not a pancreatic one. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of protein digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: low fecal elastase is a marker of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: pancreatic enzyme replacement treats both steatorrhea and creatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactose is hydrolyzed by brush-border lactase, not by pancre","C":"Pepsinogen is activated by low gastric pH, a step independen","D":"Dipeptide and tripeptide uptake uses apical H+-dependent cot","E":"Amino acids exit across the basolateral membrane by facilita"}},{"id":"sdl26_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has taken high-dose omeprazole for 2 years for reflux esophagitis. Pulse is 70/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Intragastric pH during monitoring is 6.0 (reference range: 1.5–3.5). Which change in gastric protein digestion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pepsin activity rises in the stomach","B":"Trypsinogen stays inactive in the duodenum","C":"Pepsinogen stays inactive in the stomach","D":"Protein absorption ceases in the jejunum","E":"Chief cells stop secreting pepsinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pepsinogen is converted to pepsin only at low pH, and pepsin is denatured above pH 5, so gastric proteolysis falls; protein absorption remains normal because pancreatic and brush-border proteases finish the job. Pepsin activity falls, not rises. Trypsinogen activation by enterokinase does not require acid. Chief cells keep secreting. Pepsin is denatured above pH 5, so its activity falls rather than rises. Trypsinogen is activated in the duodenum by brush-border enterokinase, not by gastric acid. Without gastric acid, protein digestion and absorption remain normal because pancreatic and brush-border proteases suffice. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors irreversibly block the parietal cell H⁺-K⁺ ATPase (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: pepsin isozymes have optimal activity between pH 1 and 3 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation and pharmacologic inhibition of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"Pepsin is denatured above pH 5, so its activity falls rather","B":"Trypsinogen is activated in the duodenum by brush-border ent","D":"Without gastric acid, protein digestion and absorption remai"}},{"id":"sdl26_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-month-old boy has failure to thrive, diarrhea, and generalized edema. Temperature is 36.8°C, and pulse is 148/min. Serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Sweat chloride is normal. Duodenal fluid has normal trypsinogen content, and adding a brush-border extract to the sample restores proteolysis. Which finding was most likely in the untreated duodenal fluid?","choices":{"A":"Low pancreatic trypsinogen secretion","B":"Low brush-border lactase activity","C":"Low pancreatic α-amylase activity","D":"Low trypsin and elastase activity","E":"Low gastric pepsin enzyme activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Congenital enterokinase deficiency leaves trypsinogen unactivated, and because trypsin activates the other zymogens, chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases stay inactive too. Trypsinogen secretion is normal here. Lactase, amylase, and pepsin do not depend on enterokinase. Normal sweat chloride argues against cystic fibrosis. Trypsinogen is present; the missing step is its activation by brush-border enterokinase. Lactase is a brush-border carbohydrate enzyme and plays no part in protease activation. Pancreatic amylase is secreted in active form, so it does not depend on trypsin activation. Pepsin is a gastric enzyme activated by low pH, independent of enterokinase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins; Disorders of protein digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypoproteinemic edema reflects low oncotic pressure from protein malabsorption (First Aid Cardiovascular, edema). COMLEX: absence of trypsin mimics loss of the pancreatic proteases (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — pancreatic proteases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Trypsinogen is present; the missing step is its activation b","B":"Lactase is a brush-border carbohydrate enzyme and plays no p","C":"Pancreatic amylase is secreted in active form, so it does no","E":"Pepsin is a gastric enzyme activated by low pH, independent "}},{"id":"sdl26_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has severe epigastric pain radiating to the back after a weekend of heavy drinking. Temperature is 38.0°C, pulse is 116/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Serum lipase is 1,450 U/L (reference range: 0–160 U/L). Premature activation of which zymogen inside the pancreas most directly starts the autodigestive cascade?","choices":{"A":"Chymotrypsinogen","B":"Proelastase","C":"Trypsinogen","D":"Procarboxypeptidase A","E":"Pepsinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Trypsin activates itself and the other pancreatic zymogens, so intrapancreatic trypsinogen activation triggers the cascade of autodigestion. Chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, and procarboxypeptidase are downstream targets of trypsin. Pepsinogen is gastric. Chymotrypsinogen is activated by trypsin, so it lies downstream in the cascade. Proelastase is activated by trypsin, so it lies downstream in the cascade. Procarboxypeptidase A is activated by trypsin, so it lies downstream in the cascade. Pepsinogen is secreted by gastric chief cells, not the pancreas. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas, autodigestion; Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: alcohol and gallstones are the leading causes of acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: lipase is more specific than amylase for acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Chymotrypsinogen is activated by trypsin, so it lies downstr","B":"Proelastase is activated by trypsin, so it lies downstream i","D":"Procarboxypeptidase A is activated by trypsin, so it lies do","E":"Pepsinogen is secreted by gastric chief cells, not the pancr"}},{"id":"sdl26_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl has had three episodes of acute pancreatitis without gallstones or alcohol use. Temperature is 37.4°C, and pulse is 96/min. Serum triglycerides are 110 mg/dL (reference range: <150 mg/dL). Genetic testing shows loss of function of a protein stored with digestive zymogens in acinar cells. Which protective mechanism is most likely lost?","choices":{"A":"Blocking of early active trypsin","B":"Enterokinase storage in acinar cells","C":"Bicarbonate secretion by duct cells","D":"Secretin release from duodenal cells","E":"Bile reflux into the pancreatic duct"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inhibitors of pancreatic trypsin are copackaged in secretory granules to block trypsin that forms prematurely, so their loss permits autodigestion. Enterokinase is a brush-border enzyme, not an acinar protein. Duct bicarbonate and secretin regulate fluid output. Bile reflux is a trigger, not a protection. Normal triglycerides exclude a lipid cause. Enterokinase is a brush-border enzyme that activates trypsinogen in the intestine, not an acinar protein. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas, why autodigestion does not occur). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SPINK1 and PRSS1 mutations cause hereditary pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: zymogen granules are impermeable to proteins, keeping enzymes out of the cytoplasm (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"Enterokinase is a brush-border enzyme that activates trypsin"}},{"id":"sdl26_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy has a photosensitive scaly rash and episodes of ataxia. Pulse is 84/min, and blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. Urine shows increased neutral amino acids, and his growth is normal. Serum albumin is 4.3 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Which mechanism best explains his normal protein nutrition despite this defect?","choices":{"A":"Absorption on basolateral GLUT2 carriers","B":"Paracellular leak of free amino acids","C":"Pinocytosis of intact dietary protein","D":"Colonic absorption of amino acids","E":"Absorption as dipeptides and tripeptides"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hartnup disease is a defect in the Na⁺-dependent neutral amino acid transporter, but most protein is absorbed as dipeptides and tripeptides on separate H⁺-dependent carriers, so nutrition is preserved; tryptophan loss causes the niacin-deficiency rash. GLUT2 carries monosaccharides. Paracellular leak and pinocytosis are not significant routes. The colon does not absorb amino acids in useful amounts. GLUT2 carries monosaccharides across the basolateral membrane, not amino acids. Intact dietary protein is not absorbable; it must first be digested to amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins); First Aid Biochemistry (Hartnup disease). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hartnup disease causes pellagra-like findings from tryptophan loss (First Aid Biochemistry). COMLEX: neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids use separate Na⁺ cotransporters (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — amino acid cotransporters","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"GLUT2 carries monosaccharides across the basolateral membran","C":"Intact dietary protein is not absorbable; it must first be d"}},{"id":"sdl26_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"In a study of jejunal biopsies from a 41-year-old male volunteer, apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange is blocked with a selective inhibitor. The bath is kept at 37°C, and the dipeptide glycylglycine is added to the luminal side. Uptake of the dipeptide falls sharply, while brush-border peptidase activity is unchanged. What best explains the fall in uptake?","choices":{"A":"Loss of the luminal sodium gradient","B":"Inhibition of brush-border peptidases","C":"Block of basolateral amino acid exit","D":"Loss of the luminal proton gradient","E":"Reduced pancreatic protease release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dipeptide cotransporters use an H⁺ gradient that the apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger creates, so blocking the exchanger dissipates the driving force. The sodium gradient drives free amino acid uptake, not peptide uptake. Peptidase activity is unchanged by design. Basolateral exit does not set apical uptake. Biopsies lack pancreatic input. The Na+ gradient drives free amino acid uptake; dipeptide uptake uses an H+ gradient. Brush-border peptidases hydrolyze oligopeptides and are unchanged here, whereas dipeptide entry depends on the H+ gradient made by apical Na+-H+ exchange. Amino acids leave across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion after apical entry, so basolateral exit does not set apical dipeptide uptake. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the apical peptide transporter uses H⁺ cotransport (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: free amino acid uptake mirrors glucose uptake by Na⁺ cotransport (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The Na+ gradient drives free amino acid uptake; dipeptide up","B":"Brush-border peptidases hydrolyze oligopeptides and are unch","C":"Amino acids leave across the basolateral membrane by facilit"}},{"id":"sdl26_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man has recurrent flank pain and passes hexagonal urinary crystals. Temperature is 36.9°C, and blood pressure is 120/78 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). His defective transporter also fails to absorb cystine, lysine, and arginine in the intestine. Which transport mechanism normally moves these amino acids into enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"Proton-dependent cotransport","B":"Primary active ATP pumping","C":"Sodium-dependent cotransport","D":"Receptor-mediated endocytosis","E":"Paracellular solvent drag"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cystinuria is absence of the Na⁺-amino acid cotransporter for dibasic amino acids in intestine and kidney. Free L-amino acids enter enterocytes by Na⁺-dependent cotransport, like glucose. Proton cotransport carries dipeptides and tripeptides. ATP pumping, endocytosis, and solvent drag are not the described routes. H+-dependent cotransport carries dipeptides and tripeptides, not free amino acids. Amino acid uptake is energized by the Na+ gradient, not by direct ATP pumping. Free L-amino acids enter on Na+-amino acid cotransporters, one each for neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids, not by receptor-mediated endocytosis. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins; Disorders of protein digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystinuria causes hexagonal cystine stones with a positive cyanide-nitroprusside test (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: unabsorbed dibasic amino acids are excreted in feces (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption by enterocytes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"H+-dependent cotransport carries dipeptides and tripeptides,","B":"Amino acid uptake is energized by the Na+ gradient, not by d","D":"Free L-amino acids enter on Na+-amino acid cotransporters, o"}},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has bloating and watery diarrhea after drinking juice with added cane sugar, but he tolerates milk. Deficiency of which brush-border enzyme is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Lactase","B":"Trehalase","C":"Amylase","D":"Lipase","E":"Sucrase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sucrase splits sucrose into glucose and fructose; without it, sucrose stays in the lumen and causes osmotic diarrhea, while lactose is still split by lactase. Lactase deficiency would cause symptoms with milk. Trehalase splits trehalose from mushrooms. Amylase acts on starch, and lipase on fat. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates; Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: congenital sucrase-isomaltase deficiency presents when sucrose enters the diet (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption). COMLEX: sucrase also contributes to digestion of starch oligosaccharides to glucose (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has a normal oral glucose tolerance test. Which transporter carries absorbed glucose out of her enterocytes into the blood?","choices":{"A":"GLUT5","B":"GLUT2","C":"SGLT1","D":"GLUT4","E":"SGLT2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Glucose and galactose leave the enterocyte across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion on GLUT2, which also carries fructose. GLUT5 is the apical fructose carrier. SGLT1 is the apical sodium-coupled entry step. GLUT4 is insulin regulated in muscle and fat. SGLT2 is a renal proximal tubule carrier. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.28). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT2 inhibitors lower plasma glucose by blocking renal glucose reabsorption (First Aid Endocrine). COMLEX: basolateral exit by facilitated diffusion follows the glucose gradient out of the cell (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with cystic fibrosis takes pancreatic enzyme capsules with meals. Which protease in the capsules digests the elastin fibers of meat?","choices":{"A":"Elastase","B":"Lipase","C":"Amylase","D":"Pepsin","E":"Colipase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Elastase is a pancreatic endopeptidase that hydrolyzes elastin fibers, and it is activated from proelastase by trypsin. Lipase, amylase, and colipase digest fat and starch or assist lipase. Pepsin is gastric, not a pancreatic product. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins); Guyton Ch. 66 (elastase and meat fibers). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystic fibrosis causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency from thick secretions in pancreatic ducts (First Aid Respiratory, cystic fibrosis). COMLEX: endopeptidases cleave interior peptide bonds, unlike exopeptidases (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":3,"stem":"A 53-year-old woman asks why her pancreas does not digest itself. Why are pancreatic proteases secreted as inactive precursors?","choices":{"A":"To speed gastric protein digestion","B":"To allow absorption as intact peptides","C":"To resist bile salt denaturation","D":"To prevent pancreatic autodigestion","E":"To permit storage in pancreatic duct cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pancreatic peptidases are stored in zymogen granules as inactive precursors and are activated in the intestinal lumen, which prevents autodigestion of the pancreas. They act in the duodenum, not the stomach. Zymogen form does not change absorption or bile salt resistance. Duct cells secrete bicarbonate, and zymogens are stored in acinar cells. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas, why autodigestion does not occur). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: premature intrapancreatic zymogen activation underlies acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: trypsin inhibitors in acinar granules add a second safeguard (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":3,"stem":"A 28-year-old man eats a high-protein meal. By which mechanism do free amino acids leave his enterocytes into the blood?","choices":{"A":"Facilitated diffusion","B":"Proton cotransport","C":"Sodium cotransport","D":"Primary active pumping","E":"Vesicular exocytosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Free amino acids cross the basolateral membrane into portal blood by facilitated diffusion; the four separate Na⁺-coupled carriers for neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids are the apical entry step. Proton cotransport is the apical route for dipeptides and tripeptides. Sodium cotransport is the apical entry route for free amino acids. Primary active pumping and exocytosis are not described. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins, Fig. 8.31). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: free amino acid entry mirrors glucose entry, with sodium cotransport at the apical membrane (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: absorbed amino acids reach the liver through the portal vein (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman chews bread slowly before swallowing. Why does salivary amylase contribute little to her overall starch digestion?","choices":{"A":"Absence of starch substrate","B":"Rapid absorption by the mouth","C":"Inactivation by gastric acid","D":"Inhibition by bile salts","E":"Need for enterokinase activation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Salivary amylase starts starch digestion in the mouth, but it plays little overall role because the low pH of gastric contents inactivates it; pancreatic amylase does most of the work. Bread contains starch. The oral mucosa does not absorb the enzyme. Bile salts and enterokinase do not regulate salivary amylase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic amylase secretion is stimulated by CCK and vagal input (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: salivary amylase activity continues inside the food bolus until gastric acid mixes in (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"grid_sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":4,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman had a total pancreatectomy for an intraductal tumor 6 months ago and has stopped taking her enzyme capsules. She has bulky stools and weight loss. Hemoglobin A1c is 7.9% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%). Which of the following sets of carbohydrate digestion findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Lactose digestion","Sucrose digestion","Starch digestion"]},"choices":{"A":["↔","↔","↓"],"B":["↓","↔","↔"],"C":["↔","↓","↔"],"D":["↓","↓","↓"],"E":["↔","↔","↔"]},"correct":"A","explanation":"Choice A (↔ lactose digestion, ↔ sucrose digestion, ↓ starch digestion) is correct. Without the pancreas there is no pancreatic α-amylase to split starch into maltose, maltotriose, and α-limit dextrins, so starch digestion falls; salivary amylase is inactivated by gastric acid and gives little help. Lactose and sucrose are already disaccharides and are split by brush-border lactase and sucrase, which are intact. Choice B (↓ lactose digestion, ↔ sucrose digestion, ↔ starch digestion) is lactase deficiency. Choice C (↔ lactose digestion, ↓ sucrose digestion, ↔ starch digestion) is sucrase-isomaltase deficiency, in which sucrose goes undigested while pancreatic amylase and maltase still handle most starch. Choice D (↓ lactose digestion, ↓ sucrose digestion, ↓ starch digestion) is diffuse villous injury, as in celiac disease, which removes brush-border enzymes. Choice E (↔ lactose digestion, ↔ sucrose digestion, ↔ starch digestion) is normal digestion, as with adequate enzyme replacement. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.27; Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (malabsorption syndromes). The ↔ cells, including starch in sucrase-isomaltase deficiency, are inferred because the sources do not grade them. Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diabetes after total pancreatectomy reflects loss of islet insulin and glucagon (First Aid Endocrine). COMLEX: brush-border α-dextrinase, maltase, and sucrase finish starch digestion to glucose (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true}],"27":[{"id":"sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes each triglyceride molecule into which products?","choices":{"A":"One monoglyceride and two fatty acids","B":"Glycerol and three free fatty acids","C":"Two monoglycerides and one fatty acid","D":"Cholesterol and two free fatty acids","E":"Lysolecithin and one free fatty acid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic lipase, secreted in active form, splits a triglyceride into one monoglyceride and two fatty acids. Pancreatic lipase does not release free glycerol, and two monoglycerides would need a second glycerol backbone. Cholesterol ester hydrolase splits cholesterol esters into free cholesterol and fatty acids, and lysolecithin comes from phospholipids. Glycerol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase cleaving triglyceride ester bonds; pancreatic lipase yields one monoglyceride and two fatty acids. Lysolecithin plus a fatty acid is the product of phospholipase A2 acting on phospholipids, not of lipase acting on triglyceride. Pancreatic lipase yields one monoglyceride, not two, from each triglyceride. Free cholesterol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on cholesterol esters, not lipase acting on triglyceride. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lingual and gastric lipases hydrolyze about 10 percent of ingested triglyceride before the chyme reaches the duodenum (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the optimum pH for pancreatic lipase is about 6, so duodenal acid must be neutralized by bicarbonate (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Glycerol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase cleaving tri","E":"Lysolecithin plus a fatty acid is the product of phospholipa","C":"Pancreatic lipase yields one monoglyceride, not two, from ea","D":"Free cholesterol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase acti"}},{"id":"sdl27_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main function of colipase in lipid digestion?","choices":{"A":"Emulsifying lipid droplets in the gastric lumen","B":"Activating trypsinogen in the duodenal lumen","C":"Displacing bile salts from lipase","D":"Hydrolyzing cholesterol esters to free cholesterol","E":"Packaging lipids into chylomicrons in enterocytes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts displace pancreatic lipase from emulsified droplets. Colipase, activated from procolipase by trypsin, displaces the bile salts and binds lipase so digestion can proceed. Dietary proteins emulsify fat in the stomach, enterokinase activates trypsinogen, cholesterol ester hydrolase acts on cholesterol esters, and enterocytes assemble chylomicrons. Trypsinogen is activated by brush-border enterokinase; colipase is instead activated from procolipase by trypsin. In the stomach, lipid droplets are emulsified by dietary proteins; colipase acts in the small intestine. Cholesterol ester hydrolase, not colipase, hydrolyzes cholesterol esters. Chylomicrons are assembled inside enterocytes, not by colipase in the lumen. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat and fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: CCK released by fat in the duodenum slows gastric emptying and gives pancreatic enzymes time to act (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Trypsinogen is activated by brush-border enterokinase; colip","A":"In the stomach, lipid droplets are emulsified by dietary pro","D":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase, not colipase, hydrolyzes choles","E":"Chylomicrons are assembled inside enterocytes, not by colipa"}},{"id":"sdl27_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic phospholipase A2 hydrolyzes dietary phospholipids into which products?","choices":{"A":"Glycerol and fatty acids","B":"Cholesterol and fatty acids","C":"Monoglyceride and fatty acids","D":"Lysolecithin and fatty acids","E":"Bile acids and free glycine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phospholipase A2, secreted as a proenzyme and activated by trypsin, converts phospholipids to lysolecithin and fatty acids. Lysolecithin then joins bile salts in emulsifying fat. The other pairs come from triglyceride or cholesterol ester hydrolysis, and bile acids with glycine come from deconjugation. Free cholesterol and fatty acid are made by cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on cholesterol ester, not by phospholipase A2. A monoglyceride and fatty acids are what pancreatic lipase makes from triglyceride, not phospholipase A2 products. Glycerol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on triglyceride ester bonds. Removing glycine from bile salts is bacterial deconjugation, not phospholipase A2 action. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile salts are amphipathic, so the hydrophilic side faces water and the hydrophobic side faces the lipid core (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the end products of lipid digestion, other than glycerol, are insoluble in water and must be carried in micelles (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Free cholesterol and fatty acid are made by cholesterol este","C":"A monoglyceride and fatty acids are what pancreatic lipase m","A":"Glycerol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on tr","E":"Removing glycine from bile salts is bacterial deconjugation,"}},{"id":"sdl27_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"In which organelle do enterocytes re-esterify absorbed lipid digestion products?","choices":{"A":"Rough endoplasmic reticulum","B":"Golgi secretory apparatus","C":"Apical brush-border membrane","D":"Lysosomal digestive compartment","E":"Smooth endoplasmic reticulum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Inside the enterocyte, monoglycerides, fatty acids, cholesterol, and lysolecithin are re-esterified on the smooth endoplasmic reticulum to triglycerides, cholesterol esters, and phospholipids. The Golgi packages chylomicrons into secretory vesicles afterward. Lipids cross the brush border by diffusion, and lysosomes digest heme, not lipids. Re-esterification occurs on the smooth, not rough, endoplasmic reticulum. The Golgi packages chylomicrons into secretory vesicles after re-esterification. Lipids diffuse across the brush border; re-esterification happens inside the cell. Lysosomal enzymes digest heme iron inside enterocytes; they do not re-esterify lipids. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chylomicrons carry dietary triglyceride and are cleared by lipoprotein lipase in peripheral capillaries (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: lipid crosses the apical membrane by diffusion, so absorption depends on mucosal surface area (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Re-esterification occurs on the smooth, not rough, endoplasm","B":"The Golgi packages chylomicrons into secretory vesicles after ","C":"Lipids diffuse across the brush border; re-esterification ha","D":"Lysosomal enzymes digest heme iron inside enterocytes; they "}},{"id":"sdl27_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"Chylomicrons leave the intestinal villus by entering which vessels?","choices":{"A":"Portal venules","B":"Villus blood capillaries","C":"Hepatic sinusoids","D":"Lymphatic lacteals","E":"Mesenteric arterioles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chylomicrons are about 1000 Å across, too large to enter vascular capillaries, so they move between endothelial cells into lacteals and reach the blood through the thoracic duct. Portal venules and villus capillaries carry water-soluble nutrients. Hepatic sinusoids and arterioles are not the exit route. Chylomicrons are too large to enter vascular capillaries, so they do not drain into portal venules. Chylomicrons are too large to enter vascular capillaries. Lymph carries chylomicrons to the thoracic duct, not to hepatic sinusoids. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: injury to the thoracic duct leaks chylomicron-rich lymph, giving milky fluid high in triglyceride (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: glycerol is water soluble and does not need micelles for absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Chylomicrons are too large to enter vascular capillaries, so","B":"Chylomicrons are too large to enter vascular capillaries.","C":"Lymph carries chylomicrons to the thoracic duct, not to hepa"}},{"id":"sdl27_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to the bile salts of a micelle when its lipids are absorbed at the brush border?","choices":{"A":"They enter the enterocyte with the lipids","B":"They are packaged into chylomicrons","C":"They remain in the intestinal lumen","D":"They are re-esterified in the cytosol","E":"They are degraded by brush-border enzymes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Micelles release lipids at the apical membrane, but the micelles themselves do not enter the cell. The bile salts stay in the lumen and are absorbed downstream in the ileum. They are not part of chylomicrons, not re-esterified, and not degraded at the brush border. Micelles do not enter the cell; only their lipids diffuse in, and the bile salts stay in the lumen for later ileal absorption. Chylomicrons contain re-esterified lipids and apoproteins; bile salts stay in the lumen. Bile salts never enter the cell, so they are not re-esterified with the lipid products. Bile salts left in the lumen are absorbed downstream in the ileum, not degraded. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mixed micelles are disks about 50 Å across with bile salts lining the outside (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile salt reuse through the enterohepatic circulation keeps the pool large without heavy hepatic synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Micelles do not enter the cell; only their lipids diffuse in","B":"Chylomicrons contain re-esterified lipids and apoproteins; b","D":"Bile salts never enter the cell, so they are not re-esterifi","E":"Bile salts left in the lumen are absorbed downstream in the "}},{"id":"sdl27_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"In which intestinal segment are conjugated bile salts actively reabsorbed?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Proximal jejunum","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Gastric antrum","E":"Terminal ileum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Conjugated bile salts are reabsorbed in the ileum and returned to the liver in portal blood, the enterohepatic circulation. About 95 percent of secreted bile acids return this way. The duodenum and jejunum are where bile salts work on lipids. The colon and stomach do not reabsorb them. The duodenum is where bile salts work on lipids; their recirculation step is in the terminal ileum. Bile salts stay in high concentration through the jejunum because reabsorption is located in the terminal ileum. The recirculation step is located in the terminal small intestine, not the colon. The gastric antrum contains pyloric glands with G cells and mucous cells that secrete gastrin and mucus, and it is not a site of bile salt reabsorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Bile; Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 7α-hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: Crohn disease of the terminal ileum can interrupt bile salt reabsorption and cause fat malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The duodenum is where bile salts work on lipids; their recir","B":"Bile salts stay in high concentration through the jejunum be","C":"The recirculation step is located in the terminal small inte","D":"The gastric antrum contains pyloric glands with G cells and "}},{"id":"sdl27_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does surgical removal of the intestinal segment that reabsorbs bile salts shrink the total bile salt pool?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic synthesis stops after resection","B":"Hepatic synthesis lags behind fecal loss","C":"Bile salts are deconjugated in the bile","D":"Gallbladder storage is lost to the colon","E":"Micelles enter the ileal wall intact"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Resecting the reabsorptive segment interrupts the enterohepatic circulation, so bile salts go out in feces. The liver increases synthesis, but synthesis falls short of the fecal loss and the pool shrinks. Synthesis rises rather than stopping. Deconjugation is a bacterial overgrowth mechanism. Gallbladder storage is unaffected, and micelles do not enter cells. After ileal resection, bile acid synthesis is strongly stimulated rather than stopped. Deconjugation is a bacterial overgrowth mechanism, not a result of ileal loss. Micelles do not enter the intestinal cells; only their lipids diffuse in. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal bile salt malabsorption from short bowel or Crohn disease can cause fat malabsorption and bile acid diarrhea (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: a smaller bile salt pool limits micelle formation and causes steatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"After ileal resection, bile acid synthesis is strongly stimu","C":"Deconjugation is a bacterial overgrowth mechanism, not a res","E":"Micelles do not enter the intestinal cells; only their lipid"}},{"id":"sdl27_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"By which intestinal level are most ingested lipids absorbed?","choices":{"A":"Mid-jejunum","B":"Terminal ileum","C":"Gastric antrum","D":"Proximal colon","E":"Rectosigmoid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most dietary lipid is absorbed by the mid-jejunum, so the work of bile salts is done long before they reach the terminal ileum for reabsorption. The ileum recycles bile salts rather than absorbing most fat, and the stomach and colon absorb little lipid. The terminal ileum is where the bile salts are reabsorbed; most ingested lipid has already been absorbed by the mid-jejunum. Most ingested lipid is absorbed by the mid-jejunum, long before the colon. Most ingested lipid is absorbed by the mid-jejunum, long before the rectosigmoid. The gastric antrum contains G cells and mucous cells that secrete gastrin and mucus, and it is not a site of dietary lipid absorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: short bowel syndrome follows extensive small bowel resection and causes malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, short bowel syndrome). COMLEX: bile salts left in the lumen after lipid uptake travel on to the ileum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"The terminal ileum is where the bile salts are reabsorbed; m","D":"Most ingested lipid is absorbed by the mid-jejunum, long bef","E":"Most ingested lipid is absorbed by the mid-jejunum, long before the r","C":"The gastric antrum contains G cells and mucous cells that se"}},{"id":"sdl27_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cells secrete intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Gastric chief cells","B":"Pancreatic acinar cells","C":"Gastric parietal cells","D":"Salivary gland cells","E":"Duodenal S cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intrinsic factor is a glycoprotein secreted by gastric parietal cells, the same cells that secrete H+. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, acinar cells secrete pancreatic enzymes, salivary glands secrete R proteins, and S cells secrete secretin. Gastric chief cells secrete pepsinogen; intrinsic factor comes from parietal cells. Pancreatic acinar cells secrete digestive enzymes; intrinsic factor comes from gastric parietal cells. Salivary juices supply R proteins, not intrinsic factor. S cells of the duodenum secrete secretin in response to H+ and fatty acids in the lumen, not intrinsic factor. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune destruction of parietal cells causes chronic gastritis and pernicious anemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: after gastrectomy, oral B12 is not absorbed and B12 must be given by injection (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gastric chief cells secrete pepsinogen; intrinsic factor com","B":"Pancreatic acinar cells secrete digestive enzymes; intrinsic","D":"Salivary juices supply R proteins, not intrinsic factor.","E":"S cells of the duodenum secrete secretin in response to H+ a"}},{"id":"sdl27_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"Free vitamin B12 released in the stomach first binds to which carrier?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic factor","B":"R protein","C":"Transferrin","D":"Apoferritin","E":"Calbindin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Free B12 binds R proteins from salivary secretions. In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins and B12 moves to intrinsic factor. Transferrin and apoferritin carry iron, and calbindin binds calcium. Vitamin B12 moves onto intrinsic factor only in the duodenum, after pancreatic proteases degrade the R proteins it first binds. Transferrin is the plasma β-globulin that carries absorbed iron, not a vitamin B12 binder. Apoferritin binds free iron inside intestinal cells; it does not carry vitamin B12. Calbindin D-28K is the vitamin D-induced Ca²⁺-binding protein of intestinal cells, not a vitamin B12 carrier. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency can impair B12 absorption as well as fat absorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: most water-soluble vitamins use sodium-dependent cotransport, unlike B12 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Vitamin B12 moves onto intrinsic factor only in the duodenum","C":"Transferrin is the plasma β-globulin that carries absorbed i","D":"Apoferritin binds free iron inside intestinal cells; it does","E":"Calbindin D-28K is the vitamin D-induced Ca²⁺-binding protei"}},{"id":"sdl27_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"What event in the duodenum allows vitamin B12 to bind intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic proteases","B":"Gastric hydrochloric acid","C":"Conjugated bile salts","D":"Brush-border lactase","E":"Salivary amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins, so B12 transfers to intrinsic factor. The B12-intrinsic factor complex resists these proteases and travels to the ileum. Acid and pepsin act earlier in the stomach, and bile salts, lactase, and amylase do not free B12. Gastric pepsin releases vitamin B12 from food, but its transfer to intrinsic factor requires pancreatic proteases degrading the R proteins in the duodenum. Bile salts do not free B12; pancreatic proteases degrade the R proteins. Lactase is a brush-border carbohydrate enzyme; B12 transfer requires protease degradation of R proteins. Salivary amylase digests starch; B12 transfer requires pancreatic protease degradation of R proteins. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia with neurologic findings (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: intrinsic factor is needed for B12 uptake in the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Gastric pepsin releases vitamin B12 from food, but its trans","C":"Bile salts do not free B12; pancreatic proteases degrade the","D":"Lactase is a brush-border carbohydrate enzyme; B12 transfer ","E":"Salivary amylase digests starch; B12 transfer requires pancr"}},{"id":"sdl27_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Active vitamin D increases intestinal calcium absorption mainly by inducing which protein?","choices":{"A":"Apoferritin storage protein","B":"Transferrin carrier","C":"Pancreatic colipase","D":"Calbindin D-28K","E":"Intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol induces the vitamin D-dependent calcium-binding protein calbindin D-28K in enterocytes, which promotes calcium absorption. Apoferritin and transferrin handle iron, colipase helps lipase, and intrinsic factor binds B12. Apoferritin binds free iron inside intestinal cells; it is not the vitamin D-induced calcium-binding protein. Transferrin is the plasma carrier of absorbed iron, not the protein that vitamin D induces for calcium absorption. Intrinsic factor is the parietal-cell glycoprotein needed for ileal vitamin B12 absorption, not for calcium. Colipase displaces bile salts to help lipase; it is not the vitamin D-induced protein. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inadequate calcium absorption causes rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: 25-hydroxycholecalciferol is the main circulating but inactive form of vitamin D (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Apoferritin binds free iron inside intestinal cells; it is n","B":"Transferrin is the plasma carrier of absorbed iron, not the ","E":"Intrinsic factor is the parietal-cell glycoprotein needed fo","C":"Colipase displaces bile salts to help lipase; it is not the "}},{"id":"sdl27_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"How do absorbed vitamins A, D, E, and K leave the enterocyte?","choices":{"A":"Bound to albumin in portal blood","B":"By sodium cotransport into blood","C":"In micelles across the basal membrane","D":"Bound to transferrin in portal blood","E":"In chylomicrons into lymph"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fat-soluble vitamins are handled like dietary lipids. They travel in micelles, diffuse into the cell, are incorporated into chylomicrons, and are extruded into lymph. Sodium cotransport handles most water-soluble vitamins, micelles do not enter the cell, and transferrin carries iron. Fat-soluble vitamins leave in chylomicrons via lymph, not bound to albumin in portal blood. Na+-dependent cotransport absorbs most water-soluble vitamins, not vitamins A, D, E, and K. Micelles do not enter the cell; the vitamins are incorporated in chylomicrons inside it. Transferrin carries iron in the circulation, not fat-soluble vitamins. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: orlistat inhibits gastric and pancreatic lipase and decreases absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (First Aid Gastrointestinal, orlistat). COMLEX: vitamin K deficiency prolongs the prothrombin time (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fat-soluble vitamins leave in chylomicrons via lymph, not bo","B":"Na+-dependent cotransport absorbs most water-soluble vitamin","C":"Micelles do not enter the cell; the vitamins are incorporate","D":"Transferrin carries iron in the circulation, not fat-soluble"}},{"id":"sdl27_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"How is heme iron converted to free iron inside the enterocyte?","choices":{"A":"Digestion by pancreatic proteases","B":"Digestion by lysosomal enzymes","C":"Hydrolysis by brush-border peptidases","D":"Cleavage by gastric pepsin","E":"Release by bile salt micelles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Heme iron crosses the apical membrane intact. Inside the enterocyte, lysosomal enzymes digest heme and release free iron, which binds apoferritin and then crosses the basolateral membrane. The other enzymes act in the lumen, and micelles carry lipids. Heme crosses the apical membrane intact and is digested inside the cell by lysosomal enzymes, not luminal pancreatic proteases. Heme is digested inside the cell by lysosomal enzymes, not by brush-border peptidases. Heme is digested inside the enterocyte by lysosomal enzymes, not by gastric pepsin. Micelles carry lipid digestion products, not heme iron. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hereditary hemochromatosis increases intestinal iron absorption through low hepcidin (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: transferrin carries absorbed iron to the liver and bone marrow (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Heme crosses the apical membrane intact and is digested insi","C":"Heme is digested inside the cell by lysosomal enzymes, not b","D":"Heme is digested inside the enterocyte by lysosomal enzymes,","E":"Micelles carry lipid digestion products, not heme iron."}},{"id":"sdl27_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman starts a weight-loss drug that blocks gastric and pancreatic lipase. One month later she has oily, foul-smelling stools. Temperature is 36.9°C, and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. Stool fat is 14 g/day (reference range: <7 g/day). Which digestive step is most directly impaired?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis of dietary triglyceride","B":"Exocytosis of chylomicrons into lymph","C":"Synthesis of apolipoprotein B in enterocytes","D":"Reabsorption of bile salts in the ileum","E":"Activation of procolipase by trypsin enzyme"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Blocking lipase leaves triglycerides unhydrolyzed. Undigested triglycerides are not absorbable and appear in the stool. Chylomicron export and apoprotein synthesis follow digestion. Ileal bile salt reabsorption and procolipase activation are not drug targets here. Undigested triglycerides are not absorbed, so the defect precedes chylomicron export. Undigested triglycerides never enter the cell, so the defect precedes apoprotein synthesis. Ileal bile salt reabsorption occurs after lipid absorption is complete and is not the blocked step. Procolipase is activated in the lumen by trypsin, not by lipase, so a lipase blocker leaves colipase activation intact. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (orlistat). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: orlistat causes steatorrhea and decreased absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (First Aid Gastrointestinal, orlistat). COMLEX: lingual and gastric lipases normally digest only about 10 percent of triglyceride (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Undigested triglycerides are not absorbed, so the defect pre","C":"Undigested triglycerides never enter the cell, so the defect","D":"Ileal bile salt reabsorption occurs after lipid absorption i","E":"Procolipase is activated in the lumen by trypsin, not by lip"}},{"id":"sdl27_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has recurrent duodenal ulcers, diarrhea, and greasy stools. Fasting gastrin is 1200 pg/mL (reference range: <100 pg/mL), and duodenal aspirate pH is 3 (reference range: 6–7). Exocrine enzyme output is normal. Which mechanism best explains his fat malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Loss of hepatic bile salt secretion","B":"Defective chylomicron assembly in enterocytes","C":"Excess colipase release from acini","D":"Reduced ileal absorptive surface","E":"Acid inactivation of pancreatic lipase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A gastrin-secreting tumor drives excess H+ secretion that overwhelms pancreatic bicarbonate. Duodenal pH stays far below the lipase optimum of about 6, so pancreatic lipase is inactivated. Bile secretion, chylomicron assembly, and ileal surface are intact, and colipase helps lipase rather than causing malabsorption. Colipase helps lipase by displacing inhibitory bile salts; it does not cause malabsorption. Bile salt deficiency impairs micelle formation, whereas this patient's steatorrhea comes from acid inactivation of pancreatic lipase. Failure to synthesize apo B (abetalipoproteinemia) blocks chylomicron formation and leaves lipid inside enterocytes, a different defect from acid inactivation of lipase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is a gastrin-secreting tumor, often of the pancreas or duodenum (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: a proton pump inhibitor reduces the H+ load delivered to the duodenum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and gastrin","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"C":"Colipase helps lipase by displacing inhibitory bile salts; i","A":"Bile salt deficiency impairs micelle formation, whereas this","B":"Failure to synthesize apo B (abetalipoproteinemia) blocks ch"}},{"id":"sdl27_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has jaundice, dark urine, and pale bulky stools. Imaging shows a mass blocking the common bile duct. Total bilirubin is 9.2 mg/dL (reference range: 0.1–1.2 mg/dL). Pancreatic enzyme secretion is normal. Which mechanism best explains his steatorrhea?","choices":{"A":"Loss of pancreatic lipase activity in the duodenal lumen","B":"Failure of trypsin to activate procolipase","C":"Failure of emulsification and micelle formation","D":"Defective apoprotein synthesis in enterocytes","E":"Obstruction of intestinal lymphatic drainage"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts emulsify fat and form the mixed micelles that carry lipid products to the brush border. When bile fails to reach the duodenum, lipid digestion and micellar transport fail. Pancreatic lipase, colipase activation, apoprotein synthesis, and lymph flow are intact. Defective apoprotein synthesis causes abetalipoproteinemia with lipid buildup in enterocytes, not bile flow failure. Trypsin activates procolipase in pancreatic juice, and his pancreatic secretion is normal, so colipase activation is intact. Lymphatic drainage carries chylomicrons after lipids are absorbed, whereas a blocked bile duct fails earlier, at bile salt delivery to the lumen. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pale stools and dark urine point to obstructed bile flow with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: bile salts are amphipathic, which lets them line micelles in the aqueous lumen (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile formation and micelles","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Defective apoprotein synthesis causes abetalipoproteinemia w","B":"Trypsin activates procolipase in pancreatic juice, and his p","E":"Lymphatic drainage carries chylomicrons after lipids are abs"}},{"id":"sdl27_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-month-old boy has failure to thrive and pale, foul-smelling stools. Blood smear shows acanthocytes. Fasting triglyceride is 8 mg/dL (reference range: 30–150 mg/dL), and duodenal biopsy shows enterocytes filled with lipid droplets. Which defect best explains his findings?","choices":{"A":"Failure of micelle formation","B":"Inactive pancreatic lipase","C":"Loss of ileal bile salt reabsorption","D":"Failure of chylomicron assembly","E":"Deficient brush-border lactase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Failure to synthesize Apo B causes abetalipoproteinemia. Chylomicrons do not form or are not exported to lymph, so lipid builds up in enterocytes and plasma lipid stays low. Lipid filling the cells shows that micelle formation, lipase digestion, and uptake into the cell are working. Bile salt uptake and lactase do not explain lipid-filled enterocytes. Lipid within enterocytes shows that micelles delivered their products to the cell. Inactive lipase leaves triglycerides undigested and unabsorbed, so lipid would not fill the enterocytes. Loss of ileal bile salt reabsorption shrinks the pool and impairs micelle formation, a step before cell uptake. Lactase deficiency causes osmotic diarrhea from undigested lactose, not lipid-laden enterocytes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: abetalipoproteinemia presents with acanthocytes, steatorrhea, and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: apoproteins cover about 20 percent of the chylomicron surface (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 5 — abetalipoproteinemia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lipid within enterocytes shows that micelles delivered their","B":"Inactive lipase leaves triglycerides undigested and unabsorb","C":"Loss of ileal bile salt reabsorption shrinks the pool and im","E":"Lactase deficiency causes osmotic diarrhea from undigested l"}},{"id":"sdl27_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man develops a milky right pleural effusion 5 days after esophagectomy. Pleural fluid triglyceride is 420 mg/dL (reference range: <110 mg/dL). He is placed on a diet in which medium-chain triglycerides replace long-chain fats. Why does this diet reduce the leak?","choices":{"A":"Medium-chain fats enter portal blood directly","B":"Medium-chain fats bypass bile salt emulsification","C":"Medium-chain fats increase lacteal reabsorption","D":"Medium-chain fats block chylomicron uptake by tissues","E":"Medium-chain fats seal the thoracic duct wall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Long-chain fats are re-esterified and packaged in chylomicrons, which travel in lacteals to the thoracic duct. Small amounts of short- and medium-chain fatty acids are more water soluble and diffuse straight into portal blood, so less lipid enters the injured duct. The other options are not mechanisms of this diet. Bypassing bile salt emulsification is not why the leak falls; medium-chain fats diffuse into portal capillaries rather than entering lymph. Medium-chain fats do not increase lacteal reabsorption; they avoid lacteals altogether by diffusing across the basolateral membrane into portal capillaries. Medium-chain fats do not block chylomicron uptake; they simply bypass re-esterification and chylomicron packaging and enter portal blood. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids); Guyton Ch. 66 (Direct absorption of fatty acids into the portal blood). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the thoracic duct carries intestinal lymph and chylomicrons into the left subclavian vein (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: chylomicrons are too large to enter vascular capillaries (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus lacteals","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Bypassing bile salt emulsification is not why the leak falls","C":"Medium-chain fats do not increase lacteal reabsorption; they","D":"Medium-chain fats do not block chylomicron uptake; they simp"}},{"id":"sdl27_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman has bloating, weight loss, and greasy stools. Tissue transglutaminase IgA is positive, and duodenal biopsy shows villous atrophy. Hemoglobin is 10.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). Pancreatic function is normal. Which mechanism best explains her impaired fat absorption?","choices":{"A":"Absent pancreatic lipase secretion into the duodenum","B":"Reduced mucosal surface for lipid diffusion","C":"Deficient apoprotein B synthesis in enterocytes","D":"Loss of bile salt conjugation","E":"Blocked lacteal drainage by tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lipid products leave micelles and cross the apical membrane by diffusion, which depends on surface area. Villous loss reduces that area and impairs fat absorption. Lipase secretion is normal, apoprotein synthesis is not the lesion, bile salt conjugation is hepatic, and no lymphatic tumor is present. Failure of Apo B synthesis is abetalipoproteinemia, with lipid trapped in enterocytes rather than lost surface area. Bile salt conjugation occurs in the liver, not in the intestinal mucosa. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease mainly affects the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum and is linked to iron deficiency anemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease gives an abnormal D-xylose test, unlike pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Failure of Apo B synthesis is abetalipoproteinemia, with lip","D":"Bile salt conjugation occurs in the liver, not in the intest"}},{"id":"sdl27_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with Crohn disease had 100 cm of terminal ileum resected. She now has steatorrhea and flank pain, and imaging shows a renal stone. Stone analysis shows a calcium stone. Serum calcium is 9.1 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains her kidney stone?","choices":{"A":"Increased colonic oxalate absorption","B":"Increased hepatic bile acid synthesis","C":"Decreased renal calcium filtration","D":"Increased intestinal calcium uptake","E":"Decreased colonic bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lost bile salts shrink the pool, so fat is malabsorbed. Unabsorbed fatty acids bind luminal calcium, leaving oxalate free to be absorbed in the colon and excreted in urine as calcium oxalate stones. Increased hepatic synthesis falls short but does not form stones. Calcium uptake falls rather than rises. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (bile). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fat malabsorption from ileal bile salt loss causes calcium to bind fat, so oxalate is absorbed and forms calcium oxalate stones (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: about 95 percent of secreted bile acids normally return to the liver (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"sdl27_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man had 30 cm of terminal ileum removed after a penetrating injury. He now has watery diarrhea several times daily without weight loss, and it improves with cholestyramine. Stool fat is 6 g/day (reference range: <7 g/day). Which mechanism best explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Undigested lactose holding water in the lumen","B":"Gastrin-driven acid overload of the duodenum","C":"Bile acids stimulating colonic secretion","D":"Bacterial deconjugation in the jejunum","E":"Loss of colonic surface for water uptake"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A short ileal resection lets bile acids spill into the colon, where they stimulate secretion and cause watery diarrhea. The liver still makes enough bile acid to keep micelles adequate, so stool fat stays near normal. A binding resin removes the irritant. Lactose, gastrin, deconjugation, and colonic loss are not the mechanism here. Undigested lactose causes osmotic diarrhea, not bile acid-driven secretion. Gastrin-driven acid overload is the Zollinger-Ellison mechanism, not a consequence of ileal resection. Bacterial deconjugation is an overgrowth mechanism causing early bile acid absorption, not ileal-loss diarrhea. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (bile). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal bile salt malabsorption can cause bile acid diarrhea as well as fat malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: longer ileal resections deplete the pool faster than the liver can replace it, adding steatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Undigested lactose causes osmotic diarrhea, not bile acid-dr","B":"Gastrin-driven acid overload is the Zollinger-Ellison mechan","D":"Bacterial deconjugation is an overgrowth mechanism causing e"}},{"id":"sdl27_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with a surgical blind loop of small bowel has bloating, weight loss, and greasy stools. A hydrogen breath test is positive soon after a glucose load. Vitamin B12 is 160 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Which mechanism best explains her fat malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Acid inactivation of pancreatic lipase","B":"Loss of the ileal bile salt carrier","C":"Failure of chylomicron export to lymph","D":"Bacterial deconjugation of bile salts","E":"Autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bacteria remove glycine and taurine from bile salts, making nonionized bile acids that are absorbed too early, before micelles finish their work. The pool reaching the lumen falls and fat absorption fails. Acid inactivation follows gastrinoma, carrier loss follows ileal disease, chylomicron failure is abetalipoproteinemia, and intrinsic factor loss does not cause steatorrhea. Acid inactivation of pancreatic lipase is the Zollinger-Ellison mechanism, not bacterial overgrowth. Loss of the ileal Na+-bile salt carrier follows ileal resection, not bacterial overgrowth. Failure of chylomicron export is abetalipoproteinemia, from failed Apo B synthesis. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bacterial overgrowth can cause B12 deficiency because bacteria consume the vitamin (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: low luminal pH also promotes early bile acid absorption by converting bile acids to the nonionized form (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"Acid inactivation of pancreatic lipase is the Zollinger-Elli","B":"Loss of the ileal Na+-bile salt carrier follows ileal resect","C":"Failure of chylomicron export is abetalipoproteinemia, from "}},{"id":"sdl27_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has fatigue and numbness in both feet. Hemoglobin is 9.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), and mean corpuscular volume is 112 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Antibodies to parietal cells are positive. Which route of vitamin B12 replacement is most reliable for her?","choices":{"A":"Oral tablets taken with food","B":"Oral tablets with antacid","C":"Sublingual tablets at bedtime","D":"Oral liquid with pancreatic enzymes","E":"Intramuscular injection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Autoimmune loss of parietal cells removes intrinsic factor, so dietary or oral B12 is not absorbed in the ileum. Costanzo states that B12 must be given by injection in this setting. Antacids and pancreatic enzymes do not replace intrinsic factor. Sublingual tablets still deliver B12 by a non-injected route, and without intrinsic factor parenteral intramuscular B12 is required to bypass the intestinal pathway. Oral B12 taken with food still depends on intrinsic factor for ileal absorption, so it is not absorbed once the parietal cells are lost. An antacid does not supply intrinsic factor, so oral B12 given with it is still not absorbed in the ileum. Pancreatic proteases free B12 from R proteins but cannot replace intrinsic factor, so oral B12 with enzymes is still not absorbed. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pernicious anemia reflects autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor from parietal cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: B12 deficiency also causes neurologic findings, which folate does not correct (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Sublingual tablets still deliver B12 by a non-injected route","A":"Oral B12 taken with food still depends on intrinsic factor f","B":"An antacid does not supply intrinsic factor, so oral B12 giv","D":"Pancreatic proteases free B12 from R proteins but cannot rep"}},{"id":"sdl27_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with Crohn disease had 90 cm of terminal ileum resected 4 years ago. He now has fatigue and paresthesias. Mean corpuscular volume is 114 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), and serum B12 is 110 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Gastric biopsy is normal. Which step of B12 absorption is lost?","choices":{"A":"Release of B12 from food by pepsin","B":"Ileal uptake of the B12-intrinsic factor complex","C":"Binding of free B12 to R proteins","D":"Gastric secretion of intrinsic factor by parietal cells","E":"Protease cleavage of R proteins in the duodenum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The B12-intrinsic factor complex is absorbed by a specific transport mechanism in the ileum. Removing the terminal ileum removes that site even though the stomach makes intrinsic factor normally. Pepsin release, R protein binding, intrinsic factor secretion, and protease cleavage all occur above the ileum. Pepsin releases B12 from food in the stomach, upstream of the ileum. B12 binds R proteins from salivary juices, upstream of the ileum. Intrinsic factor is secreted by gastric parietal cells, not by the ileum. R proteins are degraded by pancreatic proteases in the duodenum, not the ileum. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intrinsic factor is needed for B12 uptake in the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: body B12 stores last years, so deficiency appears long after resection (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pepsin releases B12 from food in the stomach, upstream of th","C":"B12 binds R proteins from salivary juices, upstream of the i","D":"Intrinsic factor is secreted by gastric parietal cells, not ","E":"R proteins are degraded by pancreatic proteases in the duode"}},{"id":"sdl27_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with chronic alcoholic pancreatitis has greasy stools and weight loss. Mean corpuscular volume is 106 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), and serum B12 is 170 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Gastric biopsy is normal. Which step of B12 absorption is most likely impaired?","choices":{"A":"Release of B12 from food in the stomach","B":"Secretion of intrinsic factor by parietal cells","C":"Uptake of the complex in the terminal ileum","D":"Degradation of R proteins in the duodenum","E":"Binding of B12 to transferrin in plasma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins in the duodenum so B12 can transfer to intrinsic factor. Without them, B12 stays on R proteins and is poorly absorbed. Pepsin release and parietal cell function are normal, the ileum is intact, and transferrin carries iron. Pepsin releases B12 from food in the stomach, a gastric step. Intrinsic factor comes from gastric parietal cells, not the pancreas. Transferrin carries iron in plasma, not vitamin B12. The B12-intrinsic factor complex resists pancreatic proteases and is absorbed by a specific ileal mechanism that is intact here; the failed step is R protein degradation. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat, fat-soluble vitamins, and vitamin B12 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: pancreatic insufficiency gives a normal D-xylose test because the mucosa is intact (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pepsin releases B12 from food in the stomach, a gastric step","B":"Intrinsic factor comes from gastric parietal cells, not the ","E":"Transferrin carries iron in plasma, not vitamin B12.","C":"The B12-intrinsic factor complex resists pancreatic protease"}},{"id":"sdl27_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man on hemodialysis has bone pain and muscle cramps. Serum calcium is 7.6 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.2 mg/dL), and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal. Which mechanism best explains his low intestinal calcium absorption?","choices":{"A":"Deficient hepatic 25-hydroxylation","B":"Deficient renal 1α-hydroxylation","C":"Excess intestinal transferrin synthesis","D":"Loss of intrinsic factor secretion","E":"Impaired chylomicron formation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The proximal tubule converts 25-hydroxycholecalciferol to active 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol through 1α-hydroxylase. In chronic kidney disease this step fails, calbindin D-28K induction falls, and calcium absorption drops. Hepatic 25-hydroxylation is intact because 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal. The other choices involve iron, B12, and lipid. Transferrin carries absorbed iron in plasma and has no role in calcium absorption. Intrinsic factor is required for B12 absorption, not calcium absorption. Chylomicrons carry lipids; Ca2+ absorption depends on 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in adults, inadequate calcium absorption causes osteomalacia (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: active vitamin D acts on intestine, kidney, and bone (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — calcium absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Transferrin carries absorbed iron in plasma and has no role ","D":"Intrinsic factor is required for B12 absorption, not calcium","E":"Chylomicrons carry lipids; Ca2+ absorption depends on 1,25-d"}},{"id":"sdl27_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has fatigue, joint pain, and darkening skin. Transferrin saturation is 78% (reference range: 20–50%), and ferritin is 1900 ng/mL (reference range: 24–336 ng/mL). Genetic testing shows a homozygous HFE mutation. Which change best explains his iron overload?","choices":{"A":"High hepcidin increasing iron absorption","B":"Loss of lysosomal heme digestion","C":"Excess intrinsic factor secretion","D":"Excess vitamin D-induced calbindin","E":"Low hepcidin increasing iron absorption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Absorbed iron moves from apoferritin across the basolateral membrane and onto transferrin. HFE mutation lowers hepcidin, which normally limits that export, so absorption runs high and iron accumulates. High hepcidin would decrease absorption. Lysosomal heme digestion, intrinsic factor, and calbindin are unrelated. Intrinsic factor is required for B12 absorption, not iron absorption. Calbindin D-28K promotes Ca2+ absorption, not iron absorption. Lysosomal enzymes free iron from absorbed heme inside enterocytes, so losing this step would reduce, not raise, iron absorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron); First Aid Hematology (hemochromatosis). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hereditary hemochromatosis is linked to HFE mutation on chromosome 6 (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: iron enters enterocytes as Fe2+ or as heme iron (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — iron absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Intrinsic factor is required for B12 absorption, not iron ab","D":"Calbindin D-28K promotes Ca2+ absorption, not iron absorptio","B":"Lysosomal enzymes free iron from absorbed heme inside entero"}},{"id":"sdl27_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has stopped taking her pancreatic enzyme capsules. She now has easy bruising and greasy stools. Prothrombin time is 19 seconds (reference range: 11–13.5 seconds), and platelet count is normal. Deficiency of which vitamin best explains her bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin C","B":"Vitamin B12","C":"Vitamin K","D":"Vitamin B1","E":"Folic acid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Without pancreatic lipase, fat is not digested and fat-soluble vitamins, which travel with lipids in micelles and chylomicrons, are malabsorbed. Vitamin K deficiency prolongs the prothrombin time. Vitamin C, B1, and folate are water-soluble vitamins absorbed by sodium-dependent cotransport. B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia. Vitamin C is water soluble and absorbed by Na+-dependent cotransport, not with dietary fat. B12 absorption depends on intrinsic factor and ileal uptake, not on fat digestion. Vitamin B1 is water soluble and absorbed by Na+-dependent cotransport, not with dietary fat. Folic acid is water soluble and absorbed by Na+-dependent cotransport, not with dietary fat. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron); First Aid Gastrointestinal (malabsorption syndromes). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency from cystic fibrosis causes malabsorption of vitamins A, D, E, and K (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: vitamin K is needed to make clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — malabsorption and pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Vitamin C is water soluble and absorbed by Na+-dependent cot","B":"B12 absorption depends on intrinsic factor and ileal uptake,","D":"Vitamin B1 is water soluble and absorbed by Na+-dependent co","E":"Folic acid is water soluble and absorbed by Na+-dependent co"}},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-week-old infant digests some milk fat before it reaches the duodenum. Which enzymes start this digestion?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic phospholipase A2","B":"Brush-border disaccharidases","C":"Hepatic lipoprotein lipase","D":"Lingual and gastric lipases","E":"Salivary α-amylase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lingual and gastric lipases hydrolyze about 10 percent of ingested triglyceride before chyme reaches the small intestine. Phospholipase A2 is pancreatic, disaccharidases act on sugars, lipoprotein lipase clears plasma lipoproteins, and amylase digests starch. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the stomach, dietary proteins keep lipid droplets emulsified because bile acids are absent (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: slow gastric emptying gives pancreatic enzymes time to digest lipid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old man eats a fatty meal. Which molecules cover most of the surface of the chylomicrons his enterocytes export?","choices":{"A":"Phospholipids","B":"Triglycerides","C":"Cholesterol esters","D":"Bile salts","E":"Free fatty acids"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Phospholipids cover about 80 percent of the chylomicron surface, and apoproteins cover the rest. Triglycerides and cholesterol esters form the core. Bile salts stay in the lumen, and free fatty acids are re-esterified. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of lipids). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Apo B is required for chylomicron formation (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: chylomicrons reach the blood through the thoracic duct (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman's liver must replace bile acids lost in her stool. Which enzyme limits the rate of new bile acid synthesis?","choices":{"A":"HMG-CoA reductase","B":"Lipoprotein lipase","C":"Pancreatic colipase","D":"Phospholipase A2","E":"Cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis from cholesterol. HMG-CoA reductase limits cholesterol synthesis, lipoprotein lipase clears plasma lipoproteins, and colipase and phospholipase A2 act in the intestinal lumen. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (bile); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Bile). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid loss in feces increases hepatic bile acid synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile acids are conjugated with glycine or taurine before secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man eats meat as his main source of vitamin B12. What releases the vitamin from food proteins in his stomach?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic factor","B":"Lysosomal enzymes","C":"Pepsin digestion","D":"Bile salts","E":"Brush-border peptidases"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dietary B12 is released from food by pepsin in the stomach, then binds R proteins. Intrinsic factor binds B12 later in the duodenum, lysosomal enzymes act inside cells, bile salts form micelles, and brush-border peptidases act in the small intestine. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 is found in animal-derived foods, so strict vegans risk deficiency (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: R proteins come from salivary secretions (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman takes oral iron for anemia. Which plasma protein carries the absorbed iron to her liver and marrow?","choices":{"A":"Apoferritin","B":"Transferrin","C":"Albumin","D":"Calbindin","E":"Ceruloplasmin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Transferrin, a β-globulin, carries iron from the intestine to liver stores and then to bone marrow. Apoferritin binds iron inside enterocytes, albumin is not the iron carrier, calbindin binds calcium, and ceruloplasmin carries copper. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: low hepcidin in hemochromatosis increases iron absorption (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: heme iron is absorbed intact and freed inside the enterocyte (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man eats eggs rich in sterol esters. Which pancreatic enzyme frees the sterol for micelles?","choices":{"A":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase","B":"Phospholipase A2","C":"Pancreatic amylase","D":"Carboxypeptidase A","E":"Enteropeptidase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase is secreted in active form and splits cholesterol esters into free cholesterol and fatty acids. Phospholipase A2 acts on phospholipids, amylase on starch, carboxypeptidase on peptides, and enteropeptidase on trypsinogen. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: free cholesterol is solubilized in mixed micelles for absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: cholesterol ester hydrolase can also release glycerol from triglycerides (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl27_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":4,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with high LDL cholesterol starts cholestyramine, which binds bile acids in the intestinal lumen. Months later he has mild bloating. LDL cholesterol is 182 mg/dL (reference range: <130 mg/dL) before treatment. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely after treatment begins?","grid":{"headers":["Fecal bile acids","Hepatic bile acid synthesis","Vitamin K absorption"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↑","↓"],"B":["↑","↑","↓"],"C":["↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↔","↔","↓"],"E":["↓","↓","↑"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Choice B (↑ fecal bile acids, ↑ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) is correct. Bound bile acids escape ileal reabsorption and leave in stool, interrupting the enterohepatic circulation. The liver raises synthesis to replace the loss, and a smaller luminal bile acid pool reduces micelle formation, so absorption of fat-soluble vitamin K falls. Choice A (↓ fecal bile acids, ↑ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) has the fecal loss backward; a binder increases, not decreases, stool bile acids. Choice C (↑ fecal bile acids, ↓ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) ignores the hepatic response to bile acid loss, in which reduced FXR feedback derepresses synthesis. Choice D (↔ fecal bile acids, ↔ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) fits a lipase inhibitor such as orlistat rather than a bile acid binder. Choice E (↓ fecal bile acids, ↓ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↑ vitamin K absorption) reverses every effect, as if bile acids were supplemented. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Bile; Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption; Fat-soluble vitamins). The vitamin cell is inferred from micelle dependence; the sources do not grade it for this drug. Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after ileal resection, hepatic synthesis rises but does not keep pace with fecal bile salt loss (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile acid loss in Crohn disease of the ileum can cause calcium oxalate kidney stones (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true}],"28":[{"id":"sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"About how much fluid enters the gastrointestinal lumen each day from diet plus secretions?","choices":{"A":"About 2 L","B":"About 4 L","C":"About 15 L","D":"About 9 L","E":"About 0.5 L"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About 2 L of dietary fluid plus about 7 L of salivary, gastric, pancreatic, biliary, and intestinal secretions gives slightly more than 9 L. Most is absorbed by the small intestine and colon. 2 L is the dietary share alone. About 4 L underestimates the load; ingested plus secreted fluid totals roughly 9 L per day. About 15 L overstates normal daily load; volumes above 10–14 L per day are reached only in cholera-type secretory diarrhea. About 0.5 L is far below the roughly 9 L of ingested plus secreted fluid the intestine handles each day. About 2 L is only the dietary fluid; secretions add about 7 L, for slightly more than 9 L in the lumen. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the 9 L absorbed daily is almost equal to the whole extracellular fluid volume, so diarrhea can rapidly deplete it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: crypt cells add secreted fluid that villus cells then reabsorb (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"About 4 L underestimates the load; ingested plus secreted fl","C":"About 15 L overstates normal daily load; volumes above 10–14","E":"About 0.5 L is far below the roughly 9 L of ingested plus se","A":"About 2 L is only the dietary fluid; secretions add about 7 "}},{"id":"sdl28_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the osmolarity of the fluid absorbed by the intestine compared with plasma?","choices":{"A":"Hyposmotic to plasma","B":"Isosmotic to plasma","C":"Hyperosmotic to plasma","D":"Variable with meal tonicity","E":"Hyperosmotic in the jejunum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The absorbate is isosmotic: solute and water are absorbed in proportion, as in the renal proximal tubule. It is not hyposmotic or hyperosmotic, and it does not vary with meal tonicity or segment. Intestinal absorbate is always isosmotic because solute and water are absorbed in proportion, so it is not hyposmotic. Intestinal absorbate is always isosmotic because solute and water are absorbed in proportion, so it is not hyperosmotic. The absorbate is always isosmotic because solute and water are absorbed in proportion, regardless of meal tonicity. The absorbate is isosmotic in every segment; the jejunum differs from the ileum and colon only in which solutes it absorbs. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: jejunal transport mechanisms resemble those of the early renal proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the small remaining volume of 100–200 mL is lost in feces each day (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Intestinal absorbate is always isosmotic because solute and ","C":"Intestinal absorbate is always isosmotic because solute and water are absorbed in proportion, so it is not hype","D":"The absorbate is always isosmotic because solute and water a","E":"The absorbate is isosmotic in every segment; the jejunum dif"}},{"id":"sdl28_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the first step in intestinal fluid absorption by villus cells?","choices":{"A":"Absorption of solute","B":"Absorption of water","C":"Secretion of chloride","D":"Secretion of potassium","E":"Secretion of mucus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Villus cells first absorb solute, and water follows osmotically so the absorbate stays isosmotic. Water does not move first. Chloride secretion is a crypt function, potassium secretion occurs in the colon, and mucus does not drive fluid uptake. Water absorption comes second, following solute absorption so the absorbate stays isosmotic. Chloride secretion is a crypt-cell function; villus cells absorb fluid and electrolytes. Potassium secretion occurs through apical K+ channels in the colon, not as the first step in villus absorption. Mucus is secreted by goblet cells interspersed among the villus enterocytes and does not drive absorption, which begins with solute uptake followed by water. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cell type determines direction, with villus cells absorbing and crypt cells secreting fluid (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: disease that reduces absorptive surface area, such as small intestinal infection, reduces fluid absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Water absorption comes second, following solute absorption s","C":"Chloride secretion is a crypt-cell function; villus cells ab","D":"Potassium secretion occurs through apical K+ channels in the","E":"Mucus is secreted by goblet cells interspersed among the vil"}},{"id":"sdl28_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"The jejunum mainly produces net absorption of which salt?","choices":{"A":"Sodium chloride","B":"Potassium chloride","C":"Sodium bicarbonate","D":"Potassium bicarbonate","E":"Calcium chloride"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the jejunum, H+ leaves on apical Na+-H+ exchange and HCO3- made by carbonic anhydrase is absorbed into blood, giving net NaHCO3 absorption. The ileum adds Cl--HCO3- exchange and absorbs net NaCl. The colon absorbs Na+ and secretes K+. Potassium chloride is not the jejunal absorbate; the colon handles K⁺, secreting it through apical K⁺ channels. Potassium bicarbonate is not absorbed in the jejunum; H⁺ is exchanged for Na⁺, so the cation absorbed with HCO₃⁻ is sodium. Net NaCl absorption is the ileal pattern, where Cl--HCO3- exchange is added; the jejunum absorbs NaHCO3. Ca2+ absorption in the small intestine depends on vitamin D-induced calbindin, not the Na+-H+ exchange that gives jejunal NaHCO3 absorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the jejunum is the major site of Na+ absorption in the small intestine (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: jejunal Na+ also enters on Na+-glucose, Na+-galactose, and Na+-amino acid cotransporters (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Potassium chloride is not the jejunal absorbate; the colon h","D":"Potassium bicarbonate is not absorbed in the jejunum; H⁺ is ","A":"Net NaCl absorption is the ileal pattern, where Cl--HCO3- ex","E":"Ca2+ absorption in the small intestine depends on vitamin D-"}},{"id":"sdl28_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone induces synthesis of apical Na⁺ channels in colonic epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Antidiuretic hormone","B":"Natriuretic peptide","C":"Cholecystokinin","D":"Aldosterone","E":"Somatostatin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Like renal principal cells, colonocytes have apical Na+ and K+ channels, and aldosterone induces Na+ channel synthesis. This increases Na+ absorption and, secondarily, K+ secretion. ADH acts on renal water channels, ANP promotes natriuresis, and CCK and secretin regulate pancreatic and biliary secretion. Somatostatin from D cells is inhibitory, suppressing other GI hormones and gastric H+ secretion, and does not induce colonic Na+ channels. CCK contracts the gallbladder and stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion; induction of colonic Na+ channel synthesis is the action of aldosterone. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic cells resemble the principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: increased Na+ entry raises basolateral pumping, which drives K+ into the cell for secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Somatostatin from D cells is inhibitory, suppressing other G","C":"CCK contracts the gallbladder and stimulates pancreatic enzy"}},{"id":"sdl28_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"Which apical exchanger is present in the ileum but not the jejunum?","choices":{"A":"Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger","B":"Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchanger","C":"Na⁺-glucose cotransporter","D":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","E":"H⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ileum has the jejunal mechanisms plus apical Cl--HCO3- exchange and a basolateral Cl- transporter. Combined with Na+-H+ exchange, this gives net NaCl absorption. Na+-H+ exchange and Na+-glucose cotransport are present in both segments. The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter is basolateral in crypt cells, and H+-K+ ATPase is gastric. Na+-H+ exchange is present in both the jejunum and the ileum. Na+-glucose cotransport is on the jejunal apical membrane, and the ileum contains the same mechanisms. The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter is on the basolateral membrane of crypt cells, not the ileal apical membrane. H+-K+ ATPase is the apical proton pump of gastric parietal cells that secretes H+ into the stomach, not an ileal anion exchanger. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the ileum, HCO3- is secreted into the lumen rather than absorbed into blood (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the ileal basolateral membrane has a Cl- transporter instead of an HCO3- transporter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Na+-H+ exchange is present in both the jejunum and the ileum","C":"Na+-glucose cotransport is on the jejunal apical membrane, a","D":"The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter is on the basolateral membrane","E":"H+-K+ ATPase is the apical proton pump of gastric parietal c"}},{"id":"sdl28_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal cells secrete chloride and water into the lumen?","choices":{"A":"Villus tip enterocytes","B":"Goblet mucus cells","C":"Paneth defensin cells","D":"Enteroendocrine I cells","E":"Crypt epithelial cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Epithelial cells lining the crypts secrete fluid and electrolytes, while cells lining the villi absorb them. Goblet cells secrete mucus, Paneth cells secrete defensins, and I cells secrete CCK. Villus cells absorb fluid and electrolytes; crypt cells are the secretory population. Goblet cells on the villi secrete mucus, whereas Cl- and water secretion is the function of the epithelial cells lining the crypts. I cells of the duodenal and jejunal mucosa secrete CCK in response to fatty acids and amino acids, not Cl- and water. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: crypts of Lieberkühn contain stem cells that replace enterocytes and goblet cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, digestive tract histology). COMLEX: normally villus cells reabsorb what crypt cells secrete (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus cells absorb fluid and electrolytes; crypt cells are ","B":"Goblet cells on the villi secrete mucus, whereas Cl- and wat","D":"I cells of the duodenal and jejunal mucosa secrete CCK in re"}},{"id":"sdl28_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which basolateral transporter brings chloride from the blood into chloride-secreting intestinal cells?","choices":{"A":"Na⁺-glucose cotransporter","B":"Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchanger","C":"Apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger protein","D":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","E":"Basolateral K⁺ leak channel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Crypt cells have a basolateral Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter like the one in the thick ascending limb. Cl- enters on it and exits through apical Cl- channels. Na+-glucose cotransport and Na+-H+ exchange are apical absorptive carriers, and Cl--HCO3- exchange is an apical ileal mechanism. The basolateral K⁺ leak channel only recycles K⁺ that NKCC1 brings in; it is not the carrier that loads Cl⁻ into crypt cells. Na+-glucose cotransport is an apical absorptive carrier of villus cells, not a basolateral Cl- entry route. Cl--HCO3- exchange is an apical ileal absorptive mechanism, not the basolateral Cl- loader in secreting cells. Na+-H+ exchange is an apical absorptive transporter and carries no Cl-. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the same three-ion cotransporter in the thick ascending limb is the target of loop diuretics (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: the Na+-K+ ATPase on the basolateral membrane keeps intracellular Na+ low for the cotransporter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The basolateral K⁺ leak channel only recycles K⁺ that NKCC1 ","A":"Na+-glucose cotransport is an apical absorptive carrier of v","B":"Cl--HCO3- exchange is an apical ileal absorptive mechanism, ","C":"Na+-H+ exchange is an apical absorptive transporter and carr"}},{"id":"sdl28_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"How does sodium reach the lumen during intestinal chloride secretion?","choices":{"A":"Passively by the paracellular route","B":"Through apical Na⁺ channels into the lumen","C":"On the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump","D":"By apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","E":"By vesicular transcytosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cl- is secreted through apical channels, and Na+ follows passively by the paracellular route between cells. Water then follows the NaCl. Apical Na+ channels and Na+-H+ exchange move Na+ into cells, and the Na+-K+ ATPase is basolateral. The Na+-K+ ATPase pumps Na+ across the basolateral membrane toward blood, not into the lumen. Apical Na+-H+ exchange moves Na+ from the lumen into the cell, the opposite direction. Apical Na+ channels are a colonic feature that absorbs Na+ from the lumen into the cell; during Cl- secretion Na+ reaches the lumen by moving between the cells. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leaky small-intestinal tight junctions permit substantial paracellular movement (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the colon has high-resistance tight junctions that limit paracellular flow (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The Na+-K+ ATPase pumps Na+ across the basolateral membrane ","D":"Apical Na+-H+ exchange moves Na+ from the lumen into the cel","B":"Apical Na+ channels are a colonic feature that absorbs Na+ f"}},{"id":"sdl28_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which covalent change does cholera toxin make to the αs subunit of Gs?","choices":{"A":"Tyrosine phosphorylation","B":"Glycosylation","C":"Ubiquitination","D":"Methylation","E":"ADP-ribosylation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The A subunit of cholera toxin catalyzes ADP-ribosylation of the αs subunit of the Gs protein. The other modifications are not the toxin's action. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates the αs subunit so Gs stays GTP-bound and adenylyl cyclase stays active (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the toxin enters crypt cells across the apical membrane and acts on Gs at the basolateral side (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the direct effect of cholera toxin on the αs subunit of Gs?","choices":{"A":"Loss of its GTP binding","B":"Faster GDP rebinding","C":"Loss of its GTPase activity","D":"Faster receptor desensitizing","E":"Gain of Gi-type coupling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ADP-ribosylation inhibits the GTPase activity of αs, so GTP stays bound and the subunit stays active. GTP binding is kept, not lost. Faster GDP rebinding would end the signal, and the toxin does not change receptor desensitization or coupling. GTP binding is preserved; GTP stays permanently bound to the modified αs subunit. The toxin prevents GTP from being converted back to GDP, so no GDP rebinding occurs. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: with GTP permanently bound, cAMP stays high and apical Cl- channels stay open (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the secreted fluid volume can reach 10 to 12 L per day (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"GTP binding is preserved; GTP stays permanently bound to the","B":"The toxin prevents GTP from being converted back to GDP, so "}},{"id":"sdl28_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Why does cholera cause massive diarrhea despite intact villus cells?","choices":{"A":"Villus cells switch over to net fluid secretion","B":"Crypt secretion exceeds villus absorption","C":"Colonic water absorption is abolished","D":"Toxin destroys the villus epithelium","E":"Bacteria invade the submucosal layer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholera stimulates crypt Cl- and fluid secretion so strongly that it overwhelms the absorptive capacity of villus cells and the colon. Villus cells keep absorbing, which is why glucose-coupled Na+ absorption still works. The toxin does not destroy the epithelium or cause invasion. Villus cells keep absorbing; the crypt secretion simply overwhelms their absorptive capacity. Colonic absorption is not abolished; it is overwhelmed by the volume secreted into the lumen. Cholera toxin acts by ADP-ribosylating Gs inside crypt cells rather than destroying the villus epithelium, and the villar cells keep absorbing but are overwhelmed. Cholera toxin enters crypt cells across the apical membrane and acts through cAMP, so the diarrhea comes from toxin-driven secretion, not bacterial invasion of the submucosa. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vibrio cholerae and some Escherichia coli strains cause secretory diarrhea through toxins (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the colon can reabsorb a maximum of about 6 to 8 L per day (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus cells keep absorbing; the crypt secretion simply over","C":"Colonic absorption is not abolished; it is overwhelmed by th","D":"Cholera toxin acts by ADP-ribosylating Gs inside crypt cells","E":"Cholera toxin enters crypt cells across the apical membrane "}},{"id":"sdl28_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"Which laxative class includes lactulose and polyethylene glycol?","choices":{"A":"Osmotic laxatives","B":"Stimulant laxatives","C":"Bulk-forming laxatives","D":"Emollient laxatives","E":"Opioid antidiarrheals"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osmotic laxatives provide a nonabsorbable osmotic load that draws water into the lumen, the mechanism of osmotic diarrhea. Stimulant laxatives act on enteric nerves, bulk-forming agents are soluble fibers, emollients lower stool surface tension, and opioid agents slow motility. Osmotic diarrhea comes from nonabsorbable solutes retaining water in the lumen; lactulose and polyethylene glycol act that way. Bulk-forming laxatives such as psyllium and methylcellulose are indigestible hydrophilic colloids that form a gel distending the colon, not lactulose or polyethylene glycol. Stool surfactants such as docusate and glycerin soften stool by letting water and lipids penetrate, and they do not include lactulose or polyethylene glycol. Opioid antidiarrheals such as loperamide slow colonic transit and increase fecal water absorption, the opposite of a laxative. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea); First Aid Gastrointestinal (anticonstipation drugs). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lactulose also treats hepatic encephalopathy because gut bacteria acidify the colon and trap NH4+ (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anticonstipation drugs). COMLEX: bacteria degrade some nonabsorbed solutes into more osmotically active particles (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"Osmotic diarrhea comes from nonabsorbable solutes retaining ","C":"Bulk-forming laxatives such as psyllium and methylcellulose ","D":"Stool surfactants such as docusate and glycerin soften stool","E":"Opioid antidiarrheals such as loperamide slow colonic transi"}},{"id":"sdl28_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the major cause of secretory diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border lactase deficiency","B":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","C":"Nonabsorbable laxative ingestion","D":"Loss of absorptive mucosal area","E":"Enteropathic bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo names overgrowth of enteropathic bacteria such as Vibrio cholerae or Escherichia coli as the major cause of secretory diarrhea. Lactase deficiency and laxatives cause osmotic diarrhea, pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption, and reduced mucosal area is a separate category. Lactase deficiency leaves lactose in the lumen, where it retains water and causes osmotic, not secretory, diarrhea. Nonabsorbable solutes cause osmotic diarrhea, not secretory diarrhea. Loss of absorptive area from infection or inflammation is its own diarrhea category, separate from secretory diarrhea. Pancreatic insufficiency impairs fat digestion and causes steatorrhea, whereas secretory diarrhea is excessive crypt fluid secretion, mostly from enteropathic bacteria. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, VIP). COMLEX: enteritis increases mucosal secretion and motility to flush the infectious agent (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactase deficiency leaves lactose in the lumen, where it ret","C":"Nonabsorbable solutes cause osmotic diarrhea, not secretory ","D":"Loss of absorptive area from infection or inflammation is it","B":"Pancreatic insufficiency impairs fat digestion and causes st"}},{"id":"sdl28_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"Diarrheal fluid is rich in which anion because intestinal secretions contain it?","choices":{"A":"Chloride ion","B":"Phosphate ion","C":"Bicarbonate","D":"Sulfate ion","E":"Lactate ion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Salivary, pancreatic, and intestinal juices have a high HCO3- content, so diarrheal fluid does too. Loss of HCO3- relative to Cl- causes hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. Chloride is retained relative to bicarbonate. Phosphate, sulfate, and lactate are not the named anion. Chloride is retained relative to bicarbonate, which is why diarrhea causes hyperchloremic acidosis. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea causes normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: diarrheal fluid is also high in K+ from flow-dependent colonic secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Chloride is retained relative to bicarbonate, which is why d"}},{"id":"sdl28_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man has a high-output jejunostomy after bowel surgery. He drinks about 2 L per day, yet stoma output is 3.5 L per day. Blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg, and BUN is 31 mg/dL (reference range: 7–20 mg/dL). Which best explains why output can exceed what he drinks?","choices":{"A":"Upper gut secretions add to dietary fluid","B":"Excess colonic secretion of potassium-rich fluid","C":"Renal water loss into the bowel","D":"Bacterial fermentation of dietary starch","E":"Stoma mucosa secreting mucus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"About 7 L of salivary, gastric, pancreatic, and biliary secretions joins dietary fluid each day. Normally the distal small intestine and colon reabsorb it, so losing them lets secreted fluid pour out. The colon is bypassed, the kidney does not empty into the bowel, and fermentation and mucus are minor. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a high-output stoma can cause dehydration and prerenal azotemia because proximal secretions are not reabsorbed (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: diarrheal loss of extracellular-type fluid lowers arterial pressure (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion adds to luminal fluid","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man receives a hypertonic enteral feed directly into the jejunum and develops cramps and watery output. Temperature is 37.0°C, and serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which process best explains the early increase in luminal fluid?","choices":{"A":"Active water pumping by crypt epithelial cells","B":"Closure of jejunal tight junctions","C":"Water entry until contents are isosmotic","D":"Toxin-driven chloride channel opening","E":"Aldosterone-driven colonic secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Water crosses the leaky jejunal epithelium osmotically, so hypertonic contents draw water into the lumen until they approach isosmolarity. Water does not move by active pumping. Jejunal tight junctions are leaky. No toxin is present, and aldosterone acts on colonic absorption. Water is not actively pumped; it follows solute so that contents stay isosmotic. Small intestinal tight junctions are leaky and allow significant paracellular movement. Toxin-driven Cl- channel opening is the cholera mechanism of secretory diarrhea, which needs a bacterial toxin that a hypertonic feed does not provide. Aldosterone induces colonic Na+ channels and increases Na+ absorption, so it does not drive secretion of fluid into the jejunal lumen. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperosmotic luminal contents draw water into the lumen until they become isosmotic (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intestinal absorbate is isosmotic, much like proximal tubular fluid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — duodenal osmolality and gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Water is not actively pumped; it follows solute so that cont","B":"Small intestinal tight junctions are leaky and allow signifi","D":"Toxin-driven Cl- channel opening is the cholera mechanism of","E":"Aldosterone induces colonic Na+ channels and increases Na+ a"}},{"id":"sdl28_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has an end ileostomy after colectomy. Her stoma effluent sodium stays close to her plasma sodium, whereas normal stool sodium is much lower. Blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. Which feature of the small intestine best explains why it fails to lower effluent sodium as the colon would?","choices":{"A":"Absent basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase","B":"Resistance of enterocytes to aldosterone","C":"High colonic potassium secretion","D":"Bile salt-driven crypt secretion","E":"Leaky low-resistance tight junctions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Small-intestinal tight junctions are low resistance and permit paracellular movement, so Na+ leaks back and a steep gradient against the lumen is hard to hold. The colon has tight high-resistance junctions and aldosterone-induced Na+ channels. The Na+-K+ ATPase is present, and colonic K+ secretion is absent after colectomy. The small intestine has a basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase that extrudes Na+ entering on coupled transporters. Colonic K+ secretion is a colonic function and cannot act after colectomy. Aldosterone induces Na+ channels in the colon, not the small intestine, whose Na+ enters on coupled transporters and leaks back across low-resistance tight junctions. Crypt Cl- secretion opens in response to agonists such as ACh and VIP and is normally reabsorbed by villar cells, so it does not set effluent Na+ near plasma. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). The effluent-sodium comparison is inferred from tight-junction resistance. Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aldosterone increases colonic Na+ absorption through apical Na+ channels (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: loss of the colon removes the high-resistance segment that finishes salt and water absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The small intestine has a basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase that ext","C":"Colonic K+ secretion is a colonic function and cannot act af","B":"Aldosterone induces Na+ channels in the colon, not the small","D":"Crypt Cl- secretion opens in response to agonists such as AC"}},{"id":"sdl28_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman has 3 days of profuse watery diarrhea from norovirus. She is weak, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Serum potassium is 2.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which mechanism most directly explains her low potassium?","choices":{"A":"Renal K⁺ wasting from loop diuretics","B":"Shift of K⁺ into cells from alkalosis","C":"Reduced dietary potassium intake","D":"Flow-dependent colonic K⁺ secretion","E":"Crypt-cell K⁺ secretion via channels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"As in renal principal cells, colonic K+ secretion depends on flow. High diarrheal flow increases K+ secretion and fecal K+ loss, causing hypokalemia. No diuretic is given, diarrhea causes acidosis rather than alkalosis, intake is not the main factor, and crypt cells secrete Cl-. Crypt cells secrete Cl-, with Na+ and water following; K+ secretion is a colonic, flow-dependent process. No diuretic was given; her K+ is lost in feces because high diarrheal flow increases colonic K+ secretion. Diarrhea loses HCO3- and causes hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis, not alkalosis, so an alkalotic shift of K+ into cells does not apply. Diarrheal fluid is high in K+ from flow-dependent colonic secretion, so excess fecal loss, not low intake, explains the hypokalemia. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypokalemia can cause muscle weakness and flattened T waves (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: aldosterone also increases colonic K+ secretion through Na+ channel synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"E":"Crypt cells secrete Cl-, with Na+ and water following; K+ se","A":"No diuretic was given; her K+ is lost in feces because high ","B":"Diarrhea loses HCO3- and causes hyperchloremic metabolic aci","C":"Diarrheal fluid is high in K+ from flow-dependent colonic se"}},{"id":"sdl28_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man has hypertension and muscle cramps. Serum potassium is 3.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), plasma renin is suppressed, and an adrenal adenoma is found. Which change in his colonic epithelium is expected?","choices":{"A":"Reduced colonic K⁺ secretion","B":"Increased opening of crypt Cl⁻ channels by cAMP","C":"Increased Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchange activity","D":"Decreased basolateral K⁺ uptake","E":"Increased apical Na⁺ channel synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Aldosterone induces synthesis of apical Na+ channels in colonocytes. Na+ absorption rises, and K+ secretion rises secondarily through increased pumping, adding to K+ loss. Decreased channel synthesis is the opposite. Crypt Cl- channels respond to cAMP agonists, and Cl--HCO3- exchange is ileal. Basolateral K+ uptake increases. Aldosterone increases colonic K+ secretion secondarily, so it rises rather than falls. Cl--HCO3- exchange is an ileal mechanism, not an aldosterone-regulated colonic one. Aldosterone increases K+ pumped into colonic cells by the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase. Crypt Cl- channels open when agonists such as ACh and VIP raise cAMP, whereas aldosterone acts by inducing apical Na+ channel synthesis in colonocytes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperaldosteronism causes hypertension, hypokalemia, and low renin (First Aid Endocrine). COMLEX: the colonic transport pattern mirrors the late distal tubule and collecting duct (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — colonic electrolyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Aldosterone increases colonic K+ secretion secondarily, so i","C":"Cl--HCO3- exchange is an ileal mechanism, not an aldosterone","D":"Aldosterone increases K+ pumped into colonic cells by the ba","B":"Crypt Cl- channels open when agonists such as ACh and VIP ra"}},{"id":"sdl28_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman takes a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor for glaucoma. Serum bicarbonate is 19 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which jejunal transport step is most directly reduced?","choices":{"A":"Apical Na⁺-glucose cotransport by SGLT1 carriers","B":"Supply of H⁺ for apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","C":"Basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ uptake","D":"Apical Na⁺ channel insertion by aldosterone","E":"Paracellular water absorption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the jejunum, carbonic anhydrase converts CO2 and H2O to H+ and HCO3-. H+ is secreted on the Na+-H+ exchanger and HCO3- is absorbed. Inhibiting the enzyme limits H+ supply for that exchange and HCO3- absorption. Na+-glucose cotransport is independent, the three-ion cotransporter is in crypt cells, and apical Na+ channels are colonic. Paracellular water absorption follows solute osmotically and does not depend on carbonic anhydrase generating H⁺. Na+-glucose cotransport does not depend on carbonic anhydrase; only Na+-H+ exchange uses its H+. The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter is in crypt cells, not part of jejunal absorption. Aldosterone-induced Na+ channels are a colonic mechanism, not jejunal. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). The drug effect on gut is inferred from the mechanism. Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbonic anhydrase inhibitors cause metabolic acidosis by reducing proximal tubular HCO3- reabsorption (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: jejunal electrolyte transport is identical to that of the early proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — carbonic anhydrase and H⁺/HCO₃⁻ generation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Paracellular water absorption follows solute osmotically and","A":"Na+-glucose cotransport does not depend on carbonic anhydras","C":"The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter is in crypt cells, not part of","D":"Aldosterone-induced Na+ channels are a colonic mechanism, no"}},{"id":"sdl28_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn has abdominal distension and fails to pass meconium. Contrast enema shows a microcolon with inspissated meconium in the distal ileum. Sweat chloride later returns 78 mmol/L (reference range: <30 mmol/L). Which defect best explains the thick intestinal contents?","choices":{"A":"Impaired apical Cl⁻ secretion by crypt cells","B":"Excess Na⁺ absorption by colonic channels","C":"Loss of basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ uptake","D":"Absent Na⁺-glucose cotransport on villus enterocytes","E":"Overactive apical anion exchange"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Crypt Cl- secretion exits through apical Cl- channels, the CFTR channel. Without it, Cl-, Na+, and water are not secreted, and luminal contents become dehydrated. The basolateral cotransporter is intact, and absorption by villus Na+-glucose cotransport is not the defect. The basolateral Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter loads Cl- but is not the apical exit step; Cl- leaves through apical Cl- channels. Colonic Na+ channel absorption is set by aldosterone; the defect here is failed apical Cl- exit from crypt cells, which leaves Na+ and water unsecreted. Villus Na+-glucose cotransport absorbs fluid, so losing it would not dehydrate luminal contents, which instead reflects failed crypt Cl- secretion. Apical Cl–HCO3- exchange in the ileum yields NaCl absorption; the defect here is failed Cl- secretion through apical channels of crypt cells. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystic fibrosis causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: Na+ follows secreted Cl- passively between cells, and water follows NaCl (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and CFTR","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The basolateral Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter loads Cl- but is n","B":"Colonic Na+ channel absorption is set by aldosterone; the de","D":"Villus Na+-glucose cotransport absorbs fluid, so losing it w","E":"Apical Cl–HCO3- exchange in the ileum yields NaCl absorption"}},{"id":"sdl28_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman has 3 L per day of watery diarrhea that continues while she is fasting. Serum potassium is 2.6 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and gastric acid output is low. CT shows a pancreatic tail mass. Which mechanism best explains her diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Nonabsorbed solute holding water","B":"Bile acid stimulation of colonic epithelium","C":"Peptide-driven crypt-cell cAMP excess","D":"Mucosal invasion by bacteria","E":"Rapid transit from gastrin excess"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A VIP-secreting tumor activates basolateral receptors on crypt cells, raising cAMP and opening apical Cl- channels. Na+ and water follow, causing large-volume secretory diarrhea that continues during fasting. Nonabsorbed solute would stop with fasting, no ileal disease is present, no invasion is suggested, and gastrin is not the hormone. Nonabsorbed solute causes osmotic diarrhea, whereas VIP drives crypt Cl- secretion through cAMP, a secretory mechanism. Bile acids stimulate colonic Cl- secretion only when they escape ileal reabsorption, as after ileal resection, which this patient does not have. Gastrin promotes gastric H+ secretion, so gastrin excess would raise acid output rather than accompany her low acid output. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37); First Aid Gastrointestinal (VIP). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (WDHA syndrome) (First Aid Gastrointestinal, VIP). COMLEX: octreotide is used to treat VIPoma (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — VIPoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Nonabsorbed solute causes osmotic diarrhea, whereas VIP driv","B":"Bile acids stimulate colonic Cl- secretion only when they es","E":"Gastrin promotes gastric H+ secretion, so gastrin excess wou"}},{"id":"sdl28_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old farmworker has vomiting, drooling, small pupils, and profuse watery diarrhea after spraying an organophosphate pesticide. Heart rate is 48/min. Plasma cholinesterase is 900 U/L (reference range: 3000–8000 U/L). Which mechanism best explains increased intestinal fluid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Excess VIP release from a pancreatic islet tumor","B":"Excess acetylcholine at crypt cell receptors","C":"Toxin-mediated Gs activation in crypt cells","D":"Nonabsorbed pesticide holding water","E":"Loss of colonic Na⁺ channel synthesis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo lists acetylcholine among the basolateral agonists that open apical Cl- channels in crypt cells. Cholinesterase inhibition lets acetylcholine build up, so Cl-, Na+, and water secretion increases. VIP excess is a tumor syndrome, ADP-ribosylation is the toxin mechanism, the pesticide is not an osmotic load, and aldosterone controls colonic channels. Toxin-mediated Gs activation is the cholera toxin mechanism, not that of a cholinesterase-inhibiting pesticide. VIP also opens crypt Cl- channels through cAMP, but here cholinesterase inhibition is present, so the agonist in excess is acetylcholine, not tumor VIP. Osmotic diarrhea requires a nonabsorbable solute in the lumen, such as undigested lactose, whereas the organophosphate acts by blocking cholinesterase. Aldosterone induces colonic Na+ channels for Na+ absorption, so a loss of their synthesis would not explain excess crypt fluid secretion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: organophosphates irreversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase and cause a cholinergic toxidrome (First Aid Pharmacology). COMLEX: atropine blocks muscarinic effects and pralidoxime regenerates acetylcholinesterase (First Aid Pharmacology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system and autonomic input","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Toxin-mediated Gs activation is the cholera toxin mechanism,","A":"VIP also opens crypt Cl- channels through cAMP, but here cho","D":"Osmotic diarrhea requires a nonabsorbable solute in the lume","E":"Aldosterone induces colonic Na+ channels for Na+ absorption,"}},{"id":"sdl28_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old aid worker has profuse rice-water stools 2 days after drinking untreated water. Blood pressure is 92/58 mm Hg, and stool culture grows Vibrio cholerae. Which intracellular messenger remains persistently high in his crypt cells?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Inositol trisphosphate","D":"Diacylglycerol","E":"Nitric oxide"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Toxin-modified αs keeps adenylyl cyclase active, so cAMP stays high and apical Cl- channels stay open. Cyclic GMP is the messenger of heat-stable E. coli toxin, not cholera. IP3 and diacylglycerol come from Gq signaling, and nitric oxide relaxes smooth muscle. Cholera toxin keeps adenylyl cyclase active, so the raised messenger is cAMP, not cGMP. IP3 is released when phospholipase C is activated, as with ACh at muscarinic receptors on parietal cells, not by the adenylyl cyclase pathway that cholera toxin keeps on. Diacylglycerol is liberated with IP3 by phospholipase C, a Gq pathway, not the cAMP pathway held active by cholera toxin. Nitric oxide is an inhibitory neurocrine that relaxes GI smooth muscle; cholera toxin keeps adenylyl cyclase active, so cAMP is the raised messenger. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin causes secretory diarrhea by permanently activating Gs (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intravenous replacement of fluid and electrolytes is the main basis of therapy in severe cholera (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — Gs/cAMP receptor signaling pathways","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cholera toxin keeps adenylyl cyclase active, so the raised m","C":"IP3 is released when phospholipase C is activated, as with A","D":"Diacylglycerol is liberated with IP3 by phospholipase C, a G","E":"Nitric oxide is an inhibitory neurocrine that relaxes GI smo"}},{"id":"sdl28_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman returns from Mexico with 4 days of watery diarrhea from toxigenic Escherichia coli. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L), chloride 114 mEq/L (reference range: 98–106 mEq/L), and bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is most likely?","choices":{"A":"High anion gap metabolic acidosis","B":"Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis","C":"Acute respiratory acidosis","D":"Hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis","E":"Acute respiratory alkalosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secretions lost in diarrhea are rich in HCO3-, so HCO3- falls relative to Cl-. The anion gap is 138 − (114 + 15) = 9, normal, which fits hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. A high gap would need unmeasured acids. Alkalosis follows vomiting, and respiratory disorders are not primary. Loss of HCO3- relative to Cl- causes hyperchloremic acidosis with a normal anion gap, not a high gap. Diarrheal fluid is rich in HCO3-, so its loss causes acidosis, not alkalosis. Diarrheal HCO3- loss causes a metabolic acidosis with a normal anion gap, not a primary respiratory acidosis. Diarrheal HCO3- loss lowers serum bicarbonate as a primary metabolic acidosis, not a respiratory alkalosis. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea is a classic cause of normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Escherichia coli enterotoxins cause secretory diarrhea like cholera toxin (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loss of HCO3- relative to Cl- causes hyperchloremic acidosis","B":"Diarrheal fluid is rich in HCO3-, so its loss causes acidosi","C":"Diarrheal HCO3- loss causes a metabolic acidosis with a norm","E":"Diarrheal HCO3- loss lowers serum bicarbonate as a primary m"}},{"id":"sdl28_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy in a flood-relief camp has had 12 watery stools in a day. He is lethargic with sunken eyes. Heart rate is 168/min, and blood pressure is 70/40 mm Hg. Which consequence of his secretory diarrhea most directly threatens his life?","choices":{"A":"Hyperkalemic cardiac arrhythmia","B":"Loss of extracellular fluid volume","C":"Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis from vomiting","D":"Bacteremic septic shock","E":"Hypernatremic seizures"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diarrhea loses extracellular-type fluid, decreasing ECF and intravascular volume and arterial pressure. Baroreceptor and renin-angiotensin-aldosterone responses fail if the loss is too great or too fast, leading to circulatory collapse. K+ is lost rather than gained, the acidosis is not alkalosis, and toxin-mediated diarrhea is not invasive. Hypernatremic seizures do not fit, because diarrheal secretions are isosmotic extracellular-type fluid whose loss shrinks volume rather than raising sodium. Diarrheal fluid is rich in K+, so the risk is hypokalemia, not hyperkalemia. Diarrheal fluid is rich in HCO3-, so its loss causes hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis rather than hypochloremic alkalosis. Toxigenic secretory diarrhea comes from crypt secretion rather than invasion, so the shock here is from loss of extracellular-type fluid, not bacteremia. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: without fluid replacement, up to 50 percent of patients with cholera die (Guyton Ch. 67). COMLEX: antibiotics can shorten the duration and volume of cholera diarrhea (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Hypernatremic seizures do not fit, because diarrheal secreti","A":"Diarrheal fluid is rich in K+, so the risk is hypokalemia, n","C":"Diarrheal fluid is rich in HCO3-, so its loss causes hyperch","D":"Toxigenic secretory diarrhea comes from crypt secretion rath"}},{"id":"sdl28_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man starts milk of magnesia daily for constipation and develops watery diarrhea. It stops within a day when he is kept nil by mouth for a procedure. Serum magnesium is 2.6 mg/dL (reference range: 1.7–2.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Peptide-driven crypt Cl⁻ secretion","B":"Toxin-driven Gs activation in crypt cells","C":"Inflammatory loss of villus absorptive surface","D":"Unabsorbed cations retaining luminal water","E":"Bile acid stimulation of colonocytes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Poorly absorbed magnesium stays in the lumen and holds water, an osmotic diarrhea that stops when the solute is stopped. Peptide-driven or toxin-driven crypt secretion persists during fasting. Villous injury and bile acid diarrhea do not fit a laxative that stopped. Peptide-driven Cl- secretion is a crypt-cell secretory mechanism; poorly absorbed solute retaining luminal water is osmotic diarrhea. Toxin-driven Gs activation is the cholera mechanism of secretory diarrhea, whereas poorly absorbed solute retaining water in the lumen is osmotic diarrhea. Decreased absorptive surface follows infection or inflammation of the small intestine, which does not fit a laxative diarrhea that stops with fasting. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea); First Aid Gastrointestinal (anticonstipation drugs). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic laxatives such as magnesium salts may be misused by patients with bulimia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anticonstipation drugs). COMLEX: secretory diarrhea results from excess crypt-cell secretion rather than inadequate absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — lactase deficiency as osmotic diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peptide-driven Cl- secretion is a crypt-cell secretory mecha","B":"Toxin-driven Gs activation is the cholera mechanism of secre","C":"Decreased absorptive surface follows infection or inflammati"}},{"id":"sdl28_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-month-old girl has 4 days of vomiting and watery diarrhea during a rotavirus outbreak. Biopsy in a similar case shows blunted villi. Heart rate is 150/min, and capillary refill is 3 seconds. Which category of diarrhea best describes her primary mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Toxin-driven crypt secretion","B":"Peptide hormone-driven secretion","C":"Nonabsorbable osmotic laxative load","D":"Aldosterone-driven colonic K⁺ loss","E":"Decreased absorptive surface area"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo classifies diarrhea into decreased absorptive surface area, osmotic, and secretory types. Infection and inflammation of the small intestine reduce the surface that absorbs fluid. Toxin-driven and hormone-driven crypt secretion are secretory types, and laxatives are osmotic. Aldosterone does not cause diarrhea. Toxin-driven crypt secretion is secretory diarrhea, a separate category from decreased absorptive surface area. A nonabsorbable solute load causes osmotic diarrhea, not loss of absorptive surface. Aldosterone increases colonic Na+ absorption and K+ secretion but is not a cause of diarrhea, whose types are decreased surface area, osmotic, and secretory. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rotavirus is a leading cause of watery diarrhea in young children and is preventable by vaccine (First Aid Microbiology). COMLEX: viral injury to villus tips can cause secondary lactase deficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"Toxin-driven crypt secretion is secretory diarrhea, a separa","C":"A nonabsorbable solute load causes osmotic diarrhea, not los","D":"Aldosterone increases colonic Na+ absorption and K+ secretio"}},{"id":"sdl28_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has had 5 days of profuse watery diarrhea. She feels dizzy on standing. Blood pressure is 96/60 mm Hg, and urine sodium is 8 mEq/L (reference range: 40–220 mEq/L). Which compensatory response is most likely active?","choices":{"A":"Suppressed antidiuretic hormone release","B":"Increased atrial natriuretic peptide","C":"Activated renin-angiotensin-aldosterone","D":"Reduced sympathetic outflow","E":"Suppressed baroreceptor reflex activity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Loss of extracellular-type fluid lowers arterial pressure. Baroreceptors increase sympathetic outflow, and the renin-angiotensin II-aldosterone system is activated to retain Na+, giving low urine sodium. ADH increases rather than falls, ANP falls with low volume, and sympathetic and baroreceptor responses increase. Baroreceptor mechanisms are activated, not suppressed, as they attempt to restore blood pressure. Volume loss lowers arterial pressure, and the baroreceptor mechanisms try to restore it, so sympathetic outflow rises rather than falls. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low urine sodium in hypovolemia reflects avid renal sodium retention (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: compensation fails if diarrheal fluid loss is too large or too rapid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"E":"Baroreceptor mechanisms are activated, not suppressed, as th","D":"Volume loss lowers arterial pressure, and the baroreceptor m"}},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with an ileostomy asks which bowel segment normally has the tightest epithelial junctions. Which segment has high-resistance tight junctions?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Jejunum","C":"Colon","D":"Ileum","E":"Stomach"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Colonic tight junctions are tight, with high resistance, and do not permit paracellular movement. Small-intestinal tight junctions are leaky and permit substantial paracellular flow. The stomach is not described in this comparison. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic transport resembles that of renal principal cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: tight-junction permeability decides whether fluid moves by cellular or paracellular routes (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":3,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has extensive Crohn disease of the small bowel. Which small-intestinal segment is normally the major site of Na⁺ absorption?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Ileum","C":"Cecum","D":"Rectum","E":"Jejunum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo names the jejunum as the major site of Na+ absorption in the small intestine, through Na+-coupled cotransporters and Na+-H+ exchange. The ileum adds Cl--HCO3- exchange. The cecum and rectum are colon. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease can involve any part of the GI tract, most often the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: Na+ entering jejunal cells is pumped out by the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":3,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy with cystic fibrosis has recurrent distal intestinal obstruction. Which protein is defective in his intestinal crypt cells?","choices":{"A":"CFTR chloride channel","B":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","C":"Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger","D":"Epithelial Na⁺ channel","E":"H⁺-K⁺ ATPase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CFTR is the apical Cl- channel through which crypt cells secrete Cl-, with Na+ and water following. The basolateral three-ion cotransporter, Na+-H+ exchange, and epithelial Na+ channels are intact, and H+-K+ ATPase is gastric. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: without crypt Cl- secretion, Na+ and water do not follow into the lumen, so contents become dehydrated (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: cAMP opens apical Cl- channels in crypt cells (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has severe rice-water diarrhea from Vibrio cholerae. Which enzyme stays persistently active in his crypt cells?","choices":{"A":"Guanylyl cyclase","B":"Phospholipase C","C":"Protein kinase C","D":"Adenylyl cyclase","E":"Carbonic anhydrase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates αs, so Gs keeps adenylyl cyclase active and cAMP stays high. Guanylyl cyclase is targeted by heat-stable E. coli toxin, phospholipase C and protein kinase C belong to Gq signaling, and carbonic anhydrase supplies H+ and HCO3-. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Secretory diarrhea, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates αs, keeping Gs active and cAMP high (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: cholera can secrete 10 to 12 L per day into the intestine (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has a pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor causing large-volume watery diarrhea despite fasting. Which drug reduces the tumor's hormone secretion?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Octreotide","C":"Lactulose","D":"Bisacodyl","E":"Psyllium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Octreotide is a long-acting somatostatin analog used for VIPoma that inhibits secretion of splanchnic vasodilatory hormones. Loperamide slows motility, lactulose is an osmotic laxative, bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative, and psyllium is bulk-forming. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (octreotide); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: octreotide is also used for acute variceal bleeds, acromegaly, and carcinoid tumors (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide). COMLEX: octreotide can cause steatorrhea and cholelithiasis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":3,"stem":"A healthy 22-year-old woman eats a normal diet. About how much water does she lose in feces each day?","choices":{"A":"About 100–200 mL","B":"About 1–2 L","C":"About 2–3 L","D":"About 500–800 mL","E":"About 5–10 mL"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Of slightly more than 9 L that enters the lumen daily, almost all is absorbed by the small intestine and colon, leaving about 100–200 mL in feces. Losses of liters per day mean diarrhea. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport, Fig. 8.34). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea can lose 9 L or more of fluid per day (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: about 2 L of daily luminal fluid comes from the diet (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo"},{"id":"grid_sdl28_q01","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":4,"stem":"A 30-year-old man in a cholera outbreak has had 2 days of profuse rice-water stools. Blood pressure is 88/56 mm Hg, and heart rate is 124/min. Stool culture grows Vibrio cholerae. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely before treatment?","grid":{"headers":["Plasma aldosterone","Serum K⁺","Serum HCO₃⁻"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↓","↓"],"B":["↑","↑","↓"],"C":["↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↑","↔","↑"],"E":["↓","↑","↑"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choice C (↑ plasma aldosterone, ↓ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻) is correct. Loss of extracellular-type fluid lowers arterial pressure and activates the renin-angiotensin II-aldosterone system. Diarrheal fluid is high in K⁺ from flow-dependent colonic secretion, which aldosterone adds to, so hypokalemia follows. Diarrheal fluid is also rich in HCO₃⁻ from intestinal secretions, causing hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. Choice A (↓ plasma aldosterone, ↓ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻) misses the volume response. Choice B (↑ plasma aldosterone, ↑ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻) ignores fecal K⁺ loss. Choice D (↑ plasma aldosterone, ↔ serum K⁺, ↑ serum HCO₃⁻) fits vomiting more than diarrhea, with loss of gastric H⁺ raising HCO₃⁻. Choice E (↓ plasma aldosterone, ↑ serum K⁺, ↑ serum HCO₃⁻) reverses each change. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Colon; Diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea causes normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, whereas vomiting causes metabolic alkalosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intravenous fluid and electrolyte replacement is the main basis of therapy in severe cholera (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true}],"29":[{"id":"sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of mechanical intestinal obstructions are caused by the four major mechanical causes combined?","choices":{"A":"About 10 percent","B":"About 30 percent","C":"About 50 percent","D":"About 95 percent","E":"About 80 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hernias, adhesions, intussusception, and volvulus together cause about 80% of mechanical obstructions. Tumors, infarction, and strictures such as Crohn disease make up the rest. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intestinal obstruction, Fig. 17.25). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 29 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: small bowel obstruction is most commonly caused by intraperitoneal adhesions, tumors, and hernias (First Aid Gastrointestinal, small bowel obstruction). COMLEX: upright abdominal x-ray in small bowel obstruction shows air-fluid levels (First Aid Gastrointestinal, small bowel obstruction).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl29_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most frequent cause of intestinal obstruction worldwide?","choices":{"A":"Adhesions","B":"Volvulus","C":"Tumors","D":"Hernias","E":"Strictures"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hernias are the most frequent cause of intestinal obstruction worldwide and the third most common in the United States. Inguinal hernias, with narrow orifices and large sacs, are most often involved. Adhesions are the leading cause of obstruction in the United States and developed nations, not worldwide. Volvulus is rare and occurs most often in the sigmoid colon, so it is not the most frequent cause of obstruction. Tumors fall in the minority of mechanical obstructions left after hernias, adhesions, intussusception, and volvulus. Strictures, such as those of Crohn disease, are an uncommon cause compared with hernias, which lead worldwide. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hernias). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: femoral hernias protrude below the inguinal ligament and carry a greater risk of strangulation than inguinal hernias (First Aid Gastrointestinal, hernias). COMLEX: acquired hernias are found in up to 5% of the population (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Adhesions are the leading cause of obstruction in the United","B":"Volvulus is rare and occurs most often in the sigmoid colon,","C":"Tumors fall in the minority of mechanical obstructions left ","E":"Strictures, such as those of Crohn disease, are an uncommon "}},{"id":"sdl29_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the segment of bowel that telescopes into the distal bowel in intussusception called?","choices":{"A":"Intussusceptum","B":"Intussuscipiens","C":"Hernia sac","D":"Mesenteric root","E":"Closed loop"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In intussusception, a segment constricted by a peristaltic wave (the intussusceptum) telescopes into the immediately distal segment (the intussuscipiens). The hernia sac is the serosa-lined peritoneal pouch that protrudes through an abdominal wall defect, not a telescoping bowel segment. A closed loop is formed by fibrous adhesions that trap sliding viscera, an internal hernia mechanism rather than telescoping. The mesentery is pulled along behind the telescoping intussusceptum; it is not the segment that telescopes. The intussuscipiens is the distal receiving segment; the telescoping segment is the intussusceptum. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intestinal obstruction, Fig. 17.25). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intussusception most commonly occurs at the ileocecal junction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception). COMLEX: ultrasound or CT of intussusception may show a target sign (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The hernia sac is the serosa-lined peritoneal pouch that pro","E":"A closed loop is formed by fibrous adhesions that trap slidi","D":"The mesentery is pulled along behind the telescoping intussu","B":"The intussuscipiens is the distal receiving segment; the tel"}},{"id":"sdl29_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"What causes most acute arterial obstruction of the mesenteric circulation?","choices":{"A":"Systemic vasculitis","B":"Mesenteric venous clot","C":"Tumor compression","D":"Thrombosis or embolism","E":"Vasoconstrictive drugs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In a large majority of cases, acute mesenteric obstruction is caused by thrombosis or embolism. Severe atherosclerosis is the main risk for thrombosis, and emboli come mostly from aortic atheromas or cardiac mural thrombi. Mesenteric venous thrombosis is a venous cause tied to hypercoagulable states, cirrhosis, and neoplasms, not acute arterial obstruction. Vasoconstrictive drugs such as cocaine cause nonocclusive hypoperfusion rather than arterial obstruction. Vasculitides are a less common cause of mesenteric arterial thrombosis than atherosclerotic thrombosis or embolism. Invasive neoplasms are a cause of mesenteric venous thrombosis, whereas acute arterial obstruction is mostly thrombosis or embolism. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk factors for acute mesenteric ischemia include atrial fibrillation, peripheral arterial disease, recent MI, and CHF (First Aid Gastrointestinal, acute mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: mesenteric venous thrombosis is an uncommon cause of ischemic bowel disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mesenteric venous thrombosis is a venous cause tied to hyper","E":"Vasoconstrictive drugs such as cocaine cause nonocclusive hy","A":"Vasculitides are a less common cause of mesenteric arterial ","C":"Invasive neoplasms are a cause of mesenteric venous thrombos"}},{"id":"sdl29_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal component is most vulnerable to ischemic injury?","choices":{"A":"Surface epithelium","B":"Crypt stem cells","C":"Basal crypt cells","D":"Muscularis propria","E":"Myenteric neurons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Capillaries run from crypt to surface before a hairpin turn, so the surface epithelium is most vulnerable while crypt stem cells are protected. Surface atrophy with normal or hyperproliferative crypts is the signature of ischemia. The muscularis propria is injured only in transmural infarction, typically from acute major vascular occlusion. Crypt stem cells are protected because capillaries reach the crypts before turning at the surface. Basal crypts receive blood first and are spared, often becoming hyperproliferative in ischemia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mesenteric ischemia causes postprandial epigastric pain, food aversion, and weight loss (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: chronic intestinal ischemia is accompanied by fibrous scarring of the lamina propria (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The muscularis propria is injured only in transmural infarct","B":"Crypt stem cells are protected because capillaries reach the","C":"Basal crypts receive blood first and are spared, often becom"}},{"id":"sdl29_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"In which age group is ischemic disease of the colon most common?","choices":{"A":"Younger than 2 years","B":"Between 20 and 30","C":"Older than 70 years","D":"Between 30 and 40","E":"Between 40 and 50"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Colonic ischemia is most common in patients older than 70 years and is slightly more common in females. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic ischemia shows a thumbprint sign on imaging from mucosal edema and hemorrhage (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: mortality in the first 30 days of colonic ischemia is about 10% with appropriate management (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is angiodysplasia most common?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum region","B":"Cecum or right colon","C":"Proximal jejunal loops","D":"Sigmoid colon segment","E":"Lower rectum and anus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Angiodysplasia is most common in the cecum or right colon and usually comes to attention after the sixth decade. The narrower sigmoid colon lacks the cecum's large diameter and high wall tension that drive angiodysplasia. Angiodysplasia arises from distention-related occlusion of submucosal colonic veins and is most common in the cecum or right colon, not the stomach. Angiodysplasia is most common in the cecum or right colon, where diameter and wall tension are greatest, not in the proximal jejunum. Angiodysplasia favors the cecum or right colon, the segment with the largest diameter and greatest wall tension, not the rectum and anus. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia presents with hematochezia from tortuous dilated vessels (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplasia bleeding ranges from chronic and intermittent to acute and massive (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The narrower sigmoid colon lacks the cecum's large diameter ","A":"Angiodysplasia arises from distention-related occlusion of s","C":"Angiodysplasia is most common in the cecum or right colon, w","E":"Angiodysplasia favors the cecum or right colon, the segment "}},{"id":"sdl29_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which test confirms angiodysplasia?","choices":{"A":"Barium swallow","B":"Stool culture","C":"Plain radiograph","D":"Angiography","E":"Breath testing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Visceral or aortic angiography (with colonoscopy) is the diagnostic test for angiodysplasia. The lesion consists of malformed, dilated, thin-walled submucosal and mucosal vessels. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (angiodysplasia); Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is more common in older patients (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplasia has a prevalence of less than 1% in adults (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"About what share of major lower intestinal bleeding episodes in older people is due to angiodysplasia?","choices":{"A":"About 1 percent","B":"About 5 percent","C":"About 20 percent","D":"About 50 percent","E":"About 80 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Although its prevalence is less than 1% in adults, angiodysplasia causes about 20% of major lower intestinal bleeding episodes in older populations. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lower GI bleeding causes include diverticulosis, angiodysplasia, and hemorrhoids (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI bleeding). COMLEX: angiodysplastic channels are separated from the lumen only by the thin vessel wall and attenuated epithelium (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What share of patients with cystic fibrosis eventually develop exocrine pancreatic insufficiency?","choices":{"A":"Under 1 percent","B":"About 10 percent","C":"About 25 percent","D":"About 50 percent","E":"Over 80 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In cystic fibrosis, intraductal pancreatic concretions cause duct obstruction, chronic pancreatitis, and exocrine pancreatic insufficiency in more than 80% of patients. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Cystic fibrosis). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat and vitamins A, D, E, and K as well as vitamin B12 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic insufficiency). COMLEX: CF pancreatic concretions can begin forming in utero (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most sensitive serologic test for celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"IgG antigliadin antibodies","B":"IgA anti-TG2 antibodies","C":"Anti-parietal cell antibodies","D":"Antimitochondrial antibodies","E":"Perinuclear ANCA antibodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most sensitive noninvasive test for celiac disease is IgA against tissue transglutaminase 2. IgG anti-TG2 can be used in patients with IgA deficiency. IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase (TG2) are the most sensitive serologic test for celiac disease, ahead of antigliadin antibodies. Antibodies to parietal cell H+,K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor mark autoimmune atrophic gastritis, not celiac disease. Antimitochondrial antibody against PDC-E2 is the characteristic finding in primary biliary cholangitis, not celiac disease. p-ANCA is found in up to 75% of ulcerative colitis patients and is not a celiac test. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac serology also includes anti-endomysial and anti-deamidated gliadin peptide antibodies (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease shows an abnormal d-xylose test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase (TG2) are the most","C":"Antibodies to parietal cell H+,K+-ATPase and intrinsic facto","D":"Antimitochondrial antibody against PDC-E2 is the characteris","E":"p-ANCA is found in up to 75% of ulcerative colitis patients "}},{"id":"sdl29_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most common cancer in people with celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"Gastric diffuse large B-cell lymphoma tumor","B":"Distal esophageal Barrett adenocarcinoma","C":"Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma","D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma of the liver","E":"Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The most common cancer in celiac disease is enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma, an aggressive lymphoma of intraepithelial T cells. Small intestinal adenocarcinoma is also more frequent. The most common cancer in celiac disease is a T-cell lymphoma of the intestine (EATL), not a gastric B-cell lymphoma. The cancers increased in celiac disease are enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma (most common) and small intestinal adenocarcinoma, not Barrett adenocarcinoma. Hepatocellular carcinoma is driven mainly by chronic hepatitis B and C infection (70% to 85% of cases worldwide), not by celiac disease. Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma is not among the cancers increased in celiac disease (T-cell lymphoma and small intestinal adenocarcinoma). Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease is associated with dermatitis herpetiformis and decreased bone density (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: a gluten-free diet may reduce celiac complications including anemia, female infertility, and osteoporosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The most common cancer in celiac disease is a T-cell lymphom","B":"The cancers increased in celiac disease are enteropathy-asso","D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma is driven mainly by chronic hepatit","E":"Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma is not among the cancers in"}},{"id":"sdl29_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"Mutation of which gene causes IPEX?","choices":{"A":"CFTR","B":"MYO5B","C":"HLA-DQ2","D":"RET","E":"FOXP3"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IPEX (immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X-linkage) is caused by germline loss-of-function mutations in the X-linked FOXP3 gene. CFTR mutation causes cystic fibrosis, impairing chloride and bicarbonate transport and leading to pancreatic insufficiency. HLA-DQ2 is the susceptibility allele of celiac disease, present in about 90% of cases. MYO5B mutations cause microvillus inclusion disease, not IPEX. Loss-of-function RET mutations cause Hirschsprung disease by halting neural crest migration, not the Treg failure of IPEX. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microvillus inclusion disease is caused by MYO5B mutations and presents with intractable diarrhea in early infancy (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: autoimmune enteropathy is treated with immunosuppressants such as cyclosporine or with stem cell transplantation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CFTR mutation causes cystic fibrosis, impairing chloride and","C":"HLA-DQ2 is the susceptibility allele of celiac disease, pres","B":"MYO5B mutations cause microvillus inclusion disease, not IPE","D":"Loss-of-function RET mutations cause Hirschsprung disease by"}},{"id":"sdl29_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which defective molecule causes abetalipoproteinemia?","choices":{"A":"Cystic fibrosis transmembrane regulator","B":"Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein","C":"Sodium-glucose linked transporter protein","D":"Low-density lipoprotein receptor protein","E":"Apolipoprotein E lipid carrier protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Abetalipoproteinemia is an autosomal recessive defect in microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP), which transfers lipids to nascent apolipoprotein B in the ER. CFTR dysfunction causes cystic fibrosis, whose malabsorption comes from pancreatic insufficiency rather than failed lipoprotein assembly. Abetalipoproteinemia results from loss of microsomal triglyceride transfer protein, which loads lipids onto apoB in the endoplasmic reticulum; the LDL receptor is not the defect. The defect in abetalipoproteinemia is microsomal triglyceride transfer protein, so apoB-containing lipoproteins are absent; apolipoprotein E is not the missing molecule. SGLT1 is the Na+-glucose cotransporter for glucose and galactose uptake, not a lipoprotein assembly protein. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: abetalipoproteinemia causes acanthocytes on the peripheral smear (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: abetalipoproteinemia presents in infancy with failure to thrive, diarrhea, and steatorrhea (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CFTR dysfunction causes cystic fibrosis, whose malabsorption","D":"Abetalipoproteinemia results from loss of microsomal triglyc","E":"The defect in abetalipoproteinemia is microsomal triglycerid","C":"SGLT1 is the Na+-glucose cotransporter for glucose and galac"}},{"id":"sdl29_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"About how many children worldwide are estimated to be affected by the enteropathy linked to poor sanitation and chronic fecal pathogen exposure?","choices":{"A":"Over 150 million","B":"Around 1 million","C":"Around 10 million","D":"Around 25 million","E":"Around 50 million"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Environmental enteric dysfunction is estimated to affect more than 150 million children worldwide and may contribute to many childhood deaths. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tropical sprue responds to antibiotics and is associated with folate and later B12 deficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, tropical sprue). COMLEX: high oral vaccine failure rates where EED is endemic suggest a mucosal immune defect (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-month-old boy has intermittent screaming episodes with his legs drawn up and red currant-jelly stools after a recent viral illness. A sausage-shaped right-sided mass is felt. What most likely served as the leading edge?","choices":{"A":"Intraluminal adenocarcinoma","B":"Inspissated meconium plug","C":"Fibrous adhesion band","D":"Hyperplastic Peyer patches","E":"Incarcerated hernia sac"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intussusception is the most common cause of obstruction under age 2. Idiopathic cases follow viral infection or rotavirus vaccine, probably because reactive Peyer patch hyperplasia forms the leading edge. An intraluminal tumor is the usual traction point in adults and older children, not in an infant after a viral illness. Inspissated stool acts as the traction point in about 1% of patients with cystic fibrosis, which this child lacks. Fibrous adhesion bands follow prior abdominal surgery or infection and are an adult cause of obstruction. An incarcerated hernia traps bowel in a sac through a wall defect and does not create a telescoping lead point. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intestinal obstruction, Fig. 17.25); First Aid Gastrointestinal (intussusception). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in idiopathic intussusception of young children, contrast or air enemas are both diagnostic and therapeutic (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Meckel diverticulum and bowel wall hematoma from IgA vasculitis are pediatric intussusception lead points (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An intraluminal tumor is the usual traction point in adults ","B":"Inspissated stool acts as the traction point in about 1% of ","C":"Fibrous adhesion bands follow prior abdominal surgery or inf","E":"An incarcerated hernia traps bowel in a sac through a wall d"}},{"id":"sdl29_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman who had a hysterectomy years ago has abdominal pain, distention, and vomiting. She has no external hernia, and x-ray shows air-fluid levels. What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Twisting of the sigmoid colon","B":"Postoperative fibrous bands","C":"Ileocecal segment telescoping","D":"Inguinal sac herniation of bowel","E":"Gallstone lodged at the valve"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Surgery, infection, or endometriosis can produce fibrous adhesions that create closed loops. Other viscera slide through and become trapped as an internal hernia, with obstruction and strangulation. Sigmoid volvulus twists a redundant sigmoid loop, whereas prior hysterectomy points to adhesions forming closed loops. Ileocecal telescoping in adults usually needs a tumor as traction point, and prior pelvic surgery instead points to adhesions. Inguinal herniation is unlikely with no external hernia; adhesion bands instead cause internal herniation. After surgery, fibrous adhesions can form bridges that create closed loops and internal herniation, which fits obstruction years after a hysterectomy better than a gallstone. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adhesions); First Aid Gastrointestinal (small bowel obstruction). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: small bowel obstruction is managed with GI decompression, volume resuscitation, and bowel rest (First Aid Gastrointestinal, small bowel obstruction). COMLEX: adhesions can follow peritoneal inflammation such as endometriosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — physiologic herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sigmoid volvulus twists a redundant sigmoid loop, whereas pr","C":"Ileocecal telescoping in adults usually needs a tumor as tra","D":"Inguinal herniation is unlikely with no external hernia; adh","E":"After surgery, fibrous adhesions can form bridges that creat"}},{"id":"sdl29_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man in a nursing home with chronic constipation has sudden abdominal distention. Abdominal x-ray shows a coffee-bean sign. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Midgut volvulus","B":"Cecal carcinoma","C":"Sigmoid volvulus","D":"Colonic ileus","E":"Ischemic colitis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Volvulus most often involves large redundant sigmoid loops, followed by the cecum. Sigmoid volvulus is more common in older adults and shows a coffee-bean sign. Midgut volvulus affects infants with malrotation, not an older adult with chronic constipation. Ischemic colitis presents with cramping left lower quadrant pain and bloody diarrhea, not a twisted loop on x-ray. Cecal (ascending) carcinoma usually presents as an exophytic mass with occult bleeding, iron deficiency anemia, and weight loss, not as sudden distention with a coffee-bean sign. Ileus is intestinal hypomotility without mechanical obstruction and shows no transition zone on imaging; a coffee-bean sign marks a twisted sigmoid loop. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Volvulus); First Aid Gastrointestinal (volvulus). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric volvulus in adults is more common with paraesophageal hernia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus). COMLEX: because it is rare, volvulus can be overlooked clinically with catastrophic results (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Midgut volvulus affects infants with malrotation, not an old","E":"Ischemic colitis presents with cramping left lower quadrant ","B":"Cecal (ascending) carcinoma usually presents as an exophytic","D":"Ileus is intestinal hypomotility without mechanical obstruct"}},{"id":"sdl29_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe abdominal pain out of proportion to a soft abdomen on exam. What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Embolic occlusion of the SMA","B":"Thrombosis of a mesenteric vein","C":"Twisting of the sigmoid mesentery","D":"Vasculitis of small serosal arteries","E":"Rupture of a cecal vascular ectasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acute mesenteric ischemia is a critical blockage of intestinal flow, often embolic SMA occlusion, with pain out of proportion to exam. Emboli commonly come from cardiac mural thrombi. Mesenteric venous thrombosis arises with hypercoagulable states, cirrhosis, or neoplasms, while atrial fibrillation favors arterial emboli. Sigmoid volvulus is a twisting obstruction of redundant sigmoid loops, not an embolic event linked to atrial fibrillation. Cecal vascular ectasia (angiodysplasia) presents with lower GI bleeding in older adults rather than sudden severe pain. Vasculitis is a less common cause of mesenteric arterial thrombosis, whereas atrial fibrillation supplies emboli, one of the two main causes of acute arterial obstruction. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease); First Aid Gastrointestinal (acute mesenteric ischemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute arterial infarction produces a sharp demarcation between normal and ischemic bowel (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: coagulative necrosis of the muscularis propria develops within 1 to 4 days of infarction (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mesenteric venous thrombosis arises with hypercoagulable sta","C":"Sigmoid volvulus is a twisting obstruction of redundant sigm","E":"Cecal vascular ectasia (angiodysplasia) presents with lower ","D":"Vasculitis is a less common cause of mesenteric arterial thr"}},{"id":"sdl29_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman recovering from septic shock has crampy left lower abdominal pain, then bloody diarrhea. A colonic biopsy is taken. Which finding best supports ischemia?","choices":{"A":"Granulomas scattered in lamina propria","B":"Crypt abscesses in diffuse colitis","C":"Villous blunting with lymphocytes","D":"Upper mucosa lost but crypts spared","E":"Thickened subepithelial collagen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ischemic injury progresses from the lumen downward because crypts receive blood first. Atrophy or sloughing of the upper mucosa with normal or hyperproliferative crypts is the morphologic signature. Villous blunting with intraepithelial lymphocytes is the celiac pattern, not the ischemic pattern of surface loss with spared crypts. A thickened subepithelial collagen band (more than 10 µm) defines collagenous colitis, which presents with watery, not bloody, diarrhea. Noncaseating granulomas are a hallmark of Crohn disease, not of ischemic colitis. Crypt abscesses in continuous colitis suggest ulcerative colitis, whereas ischemia shows surface loss with spared crypts. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic ischemia commonly occurs at the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: neutrophils are recruited into ischemic mucosa within hours of reperfusion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Villous blunting with intraepithelial lymphocytes is the cel","E":"A thickened subepithelial collagen band (more than 10 µm) de","A":"Noncaseating granulomas are a hallmark of Crohn disease, not","B":"Crypt abscesses in continuous colitis suggest ulcerative col"}},{"id":"sdl29_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man has ischemic colitis confined to the right colon. Why does this location carry a worse prognosis?","choices":{"A":"The right colon wall is especially thin","B":"The right colon lacks a submucosal layer","C":"The right colon has no collateral arcades","D":"Bacteria are far denser in the right colon","E":"Its artery also feeds the small intestine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mortality is doubled in right-sided colonic ischemia. The right colon is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery, which also supplies much of the small intestine, so right-sided disease may signal wider compromise. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mesenteric ischemia most commonly involves the SMA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: COPD and symptoms lasting more than 2 weeks are poor prognostic signs in colonic ischemia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with aortic stenosis has recurrent painless hematochezia and iron deficiency. Colonoscopy finds small flat red mucosal lesions. What will histology most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Glands showing high-grade dysplasia","B":"Granulation tissue in an ulcer bed","C":"Ectatic nests of thin-walled vessels","D":"Mucosal outpouching through the muscle","E":"Noncaseating granulomas in the mucosa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Angiodysplasia consists of ectatic nests of tortuous veins, venules, and capillaries, separated from the lumen only by a thin wall and attenuated epithelium. Minor injury makes them bleed. Mucosal outpouching through the muscle wall describes a diverticulum, whereas angiodysplasia shows ectatic nests of tortuous veins, venules, and capillaries. Angiodysplasia histology shows ectatic nests of tortuous thin-walled vessels; granulomas are not a feature. Dysplasia is the feature of adenomas and precancerous lesions, not of the vascular ectasia of angiodysplasia. Granulation tissue forms the base of a peptic ulcer, whereas angiodysplasia shows ectatic thin-walled vessels. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia); First Aid Gastrointestinal (angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: false diverticula occur where vasa recta penetrate the muscularis externa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, diverticula of the GI tract). COMLEX: angiodysplasia usually comes to clinical attention after the sixth decade (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Mucosal outpouching through the muscle wall describes a dive","E":"Angiodysplasia histology shows ectatic nests of tortuous thi","A":"Dysplasia is the feature of adenomas and precancerous lesion","B":"Granulation tissue forms the base of a peptic ulcer, whereas"}},{"id":"sdl29_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old man has bleeding from colonic angiodysplasia. Which mechanical factor is thought to explain its typical location?","choices":{"A":"Lowest blood flow at a watershed zone","B":"Thinnest muscularis of any colonic part","C":"Highest bacterial count in the colon lumen","D":"Largest diameter gives most wall tension","E":"Most lymphoid tissue in the large bowel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because the cecum has the largest diameter of any colonic segment, it develops the greatest wall tension. This may explain the cecal and right-colon predominance of angiodysplasia. Watershed zones are the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid, which explain ischemic colitis sites rather than cecal angiodysplasia. The cecal location of angiodysplasia is explained by its having the largest diameter and so the greatest wall tension, not by a thinner muscularis. The proposed mechanism for angiodysplasia is mechanical: distention and contraction occlude submucosal veins, worst in the wide cecum. Bacterial load plays no part. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia in older patients is associated with aortic stenosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: the pathogenesis of angiodysplasia has been attributed to mechanical and congenital factors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Watershed zones are the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid, wh","B":"The cecal location of angiodysplasia is explained by its hav","C":"The proposed mechanism for angiodysplasia is mechanical: dis"}},{"id":"sdl29_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man has recurrent hematochezia from colonic angiodysplasia. Which of his conditions is classically associated with this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Type 2 diabetes mellitus","B":"End-stage renal disease","C":"Primary hypothyroidism","D":"Gouty arthritis history","E":"Chronic obstructive lung"},"correct":"B","explanation":"First Aid associates angiodysplasia with end-stage renal disease, von Willebrand disease, and aortic stenosis. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (angiodysplasia); Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is confirmed by angiography (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: normal gut distention may intermittently occlude submucosal veins crossing the muscularis propria (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has bloating, iron deficiency anemia, and an itchy blistering rash on her elbows. A biopsy of the second part of the duodenum is taken. What will it most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Lymphocytosis with villous atrophy","B":"Vacuolated enterocytes filled with lipid","C":"Foamy macrophages packed in the lamina","D":"Transmural granulomas with deep fissuring","E":"Normal villi without inflammatory cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Celiac disease shows increased intraepithelial CD8+ T cells, crypt hyperplasia, and villous atrophy, best seen in the second part of the duodenum or proximal jejunum. Dermatitis herpetiformis occurs in up to 10% of patients. Lipid-vacuolated enterocytes are the abetalipoproteinemia pattern, from triglyceride that MTP defects leave trapped in cells. Normal villi fit lactase deficiency, a purely biochemical defect, not celiac disease with dermatitis herpetiformis. Lamina propria packed with PAS-positive foamy macrophages is the picture of Whipple disease, not celiac disease with dermatitis herpetiformis. Noncaseating granulomas in any layer of a transmurally inflamed bowel wall are a hallmark of Crohn disease, not celiac disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease); First Aid Gastrointestinal (celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease is associated with HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease in adults most commonly presents between ages 30 and 60 (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lipid-vacuolated enterocytes are the abetalipoproteinemia pa","E":"Normal villi fit lactase deficiency, a purely biochemical de","C":"Lamina propria packed with PAS-positive foamy macrophages is","D":"Noncaseating granulomas in any layer of a transmurally infla"}},{"id":"sdl29_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man with chronic diarrhea has suspected gluten-sensitive enteropathy, and HLA-DQ typing is ordered. How is this genetic result most useful?","choices":{"A":"Positive result confirms the diagnosis","B":"Result predicts the lymphoma risk","C":"Result sets the diet duration needed","D":"Result replaces the need for biopsy","E":"Negative result argues against it"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HLA-DQ2 is present in about 90% of patients, with DQ8 in most of the rest. Their absence has negative predictive value, but their presence does not confirm the diagnosis. A positive HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 result does not confirm celiac disease, so it cannot replace serology and biopsy; its value is in a negative result. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac concordance in monozygotic twins is about 75% (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: celiac disease is associated with type 1 diabetes, thyroiditis, Sjögren syndrome, and IgA nephropathy (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"A positive HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 result does not confirm celiac dis","A":"A positive HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 result does not confirm celiac dis"}},{"id":"sdl29_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has bulky greasy stools and poor weight gain. Which phase of nutrient absorption is primarily impaired?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border digestion","B":"Intraluminal digestion","C":"Transepithelial transport","D":"Lymphatic lipid transport","E":"Mucosal nutrient uptake"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cystic fibrosis causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, which impairs the intraluminal phase of absorption. Oral enzyme supplements treat it. Brush-border (terminal) digestion is the phase lost in lactase deficiency, not pancreatic insufficiency. Cystic fibrosis causes pancreatic exocrine insufficiency, which impairs the intraluminal digestion phase, not transepithelial transport. Lymphatic transport is the last absorptive phase, and it is impaired by lymphatic obstruction rather than by the pancreatic insufficiency of cystic fibrosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Cystic fibrosis); Robbins Ch. 17 (Malabsorption and diarrhea, Table 17.6). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency gives a normal d-xylose test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic insufficiency). COMLEX: malabsorption can disturb intraluminal digestion, terminal digestion, transepithelial transport, or lymphatic transport (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and CFTR","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Brush-border (terminal) digestion is the phase lost in lacta","C":"Cystic fibrosis causes pancreatic exocrine insufficiency, wh","D":"Lymphatic transport is the last absorptive phase, and it is "}},{"id":"sdl29_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-month-old girl has steatorrhea and failure to thrive. Her blood smear shows acanthocytes, and plasma lacks apoB-containing lipoproteins. What will a small-bowel biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Lipid-vacuolated enterocytes","B":"Flattened villi with lymphocytes","C":"Neutrophils within the crypts","D":"PAS-positive foamy macrophages","E":"Absent ganglion cell clusters"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In abetalipoproteinemia, lipids are not assembled into lipoproteins and accumulate in enterocytes. The triglyceride shows as epithelial vacuolization, highlighted by oil red O after a fatty meal. Flattened villi with intraepithelial lymphocytes describe celiac disease, not failed lipoprotein assembly. Neutrophilic mucosal infiltration characterizes IPEX autoimmune enteropathy rather than abetalipoproteinemia. PAS-positive foamy macrophages are seen in Whipple disease, whereas abetalipoproteinemia shows enterocytes vacuolated by lipid. Absent submucosal and myenteric ganglion cells define Hirschsprung disease, which causes functional obstruction rather than lipid-laden enterocytes. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: failure to absorb essential fatty acids in abetalipoproteinemia causes fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: abetalipoproteinemia is autosomal recessive (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Flattened villi with intraepithelial lymphocytes describe ce","C":"Neutrophilic mucosal infiltration characterizes IPEX autoimm","D":"PAS-positive foamy macrophages are seen in Whipple disease, ","E":"Absent submucosal and myenteric ganglion cells define Hirsch"}},{"id":"sdl29_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has severe persistent diarrhea, type 1 diabetes, and eczema. His maternal uncle died in infancy with similar findings. Which cell type is defective?","choices":{"A":"Intraepithelial CD8+ T cells","B":"Mature B lymphocytes","C":"CD4+ regulatory T cells","D":"Intestinal goblet cells","E":"Small-bowel enterocytes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IPEX is an X-linked autoimmune enteropathy in which loss of FOXP3, a transcription factor of CD4+ regulatory T cells, impairs their development and function. Neutrophils often infiltrate the mucosa, unlike celiac disease. Intraepithelial CD8+ T cells are the cytotoxic effectors that destroy enterocytes in celiac disease, not the FOXP3-dependent cells. Goblet cells are targets of the autoantibodies in IPEX, not the cells whose development fails. Enterocytes are attacked by autoantibodies in IPEX but are not the cell type that FOXP3 loss disables. IPEX is caused by mutations in FOXP3, a transcription factor needed by CD4+ regulatory T cells; B cells are not the defective population. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoantibodies to enterocytes and goblet cells are common in autoimmune enteropathy (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: intraepithelial lymphocytes in autoimmune enteropathy increase less than in celiac disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Intraepithelial CD8+ T cells are the cytotoxic effectors tha","D":"Goblet cells are targets of the autoantibodies in IPEX, not ","E":"Enterocytes are attacked by autoantibodies in IPEX but are n","B":"IPEX is caused by mutations in FOXP3, a transcription factor"}},{"id":"sdl29_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl from a rural community with poor sanitation in Zambia has stunted growth and malabsorption. No single pathogen is found, and antibiotics do not help. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Classic gluten-sensitive enteropathy","B":"Microvillus inclusion disease","C":"Congenital lipoprotein deficiency","D":"Inherited autoimmune enteropathy","E":"Environmental enteric dysfunction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Environmental enteric dysfunction occurs where sanitation is poor. It likely reflects chronic fecal pathogen exposure and repeated early diarrhea, no single agent is implicated, and antibiotics and supplements do not reverse it. Gluten-sensitive enteropathy is HLA-DQ2/DQ8-linked and shows more advanced villous atrophy than environmental enteric dysfunction. Congenital lipoprotein deficiency (abetalipoproteinemia) is an autosomal recessive MTP defect with acanthocytes, not a sanitation-linked enteropathy. Inherited autoimmune enteropathy (IPEX) is an X-linked FOXP3 defect with polyendocrinopathy, not an environmental disorder. Microvillus inclusion disease is an inherited MYO5B disorder that starts before 3 months of age, unlike a sanitation-linked enteropathy in a 3-year-old. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biopsies in environmental enteric dysfunction show more immune cell infiltration and less villous atrophy than advanced celiac disease (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: stunting from environmental enteric dysfunction may be accompanied by uncorrectable cognitive deficits (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gluten-sensitive enteropathy is HLA-DQ2/DQ8-linked and shows","C":"Congenital lipoprotein deficiency (abetalipoproteinemia) is ","D":"Inherited autoimmune enteropathy (IPEX) is an X-linked FOXP3","B":"Microvillus inclusion disease is an inherited MYO5B disorder"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a painful, irreducible inguinal bulge. At the neck of the sac, what is compromised first?","choices":{"A":"Arterial inflow","B":"Lymph drainage","C":"Vagal supply","D":"Peritoneal fluid","E":"Venous drainage"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pressure at the neck of a hernia sac first impairs venous drainage. Stasis and edema then cause incarceration, then arterial and venous compromise (strangulation) and infarction. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hernias, Fig. 17.26). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inguinal hernias tend to have narrow orifices and large sacs (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: complicated hernias can present with tenderness, erythema, and fever (First Aid Gastrointestinal, hernias).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — physiologic herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has her SMA blood flow restored after mesenteric thrombosis. When does most bowel damage occur?","choices":{"A":"Before the clot","B":"During reperfusion","C":"At first hypoxia","D":"After the scarring","E":"During fasting"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ischemic injury occurs in two phases: initial hypoxic injury, then reperfusion, when blood supply returns and the greatest damage occurs. Bacterial products, free radicals, and neutrophils contribute. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: emboli causing mesenteric occlusion most often arise from aortic atheromas or cardiac mural thrombi (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: necrotizing enterocolitis can cause pneumatosis intestinalis and portal venous gas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, necrotizing enterocolitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 77-year-old man has bleeding from colonic vascular ectasias. What process likely produced them?","choices":{"A":"Repeated vein occlusion","B":"Portal hypertension","C":"Inherited telangiectasia","D":"Radiation damage","E":"Embolic infarction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Normal distention and contraction may intermittently occlude submucosal veins crossing the muscularis propria. This leads to focal dilation and tortuosity of overlying vessels. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is associated with von Willebrand disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplastic vessels are vulnerable to disruption by even minor injury (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with celiac disease has new pain and weight loss despite a strict gluten-free diet. What should be suspected?","choices":{"A":"Lactose overload","B":"Dietary relapse","C":"Bile salt excess","D":"Refractory sprue","E":"Viral enteritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pain, diarrhea, and weight loss despite a strict gluten-free diet raise concern for refractory sprue, a possible precursor of T-cell lymphoma, or for cancer. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a gluten-free diet usually restores near-normal histology within 6 to 24 months (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: celiac disease primarily affects the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old Chinese man has bloating and flatulence after milk. What will a duodenal biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Flattened villi","B":"Lipid vacuoles","C":"Unremarkable mucosa","D":"Crypt abscesses","E":"Foamy macrophages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acquired lactose intolerance comes from age-related downregulation of gene expression. Because the defect is biochemical, histology is generally unremarkable. Flattened villi indicate the villous atrophy of celiac disease. Lipid vacuoles in enterocytes are the triglyceride accumulation of abetalipoproteinemia. Neutrophilic crypt abscesses are a feature of ulcerative colitis. Distended foamy macrophages in the lamina propria indicate Whipple disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Lactase deficiency). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lactose hydrogen breath test is positive if breath hydrogen rises more than 20 ppm over baseline (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: congenital lactose intolerance is a rare autosomal recessive disorder (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion by amylase and brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Flattened villi indicate the villous atrophy of celiac disea","B":"Lipid vacuoles in enterocytes are the triglyceride accumulat","D":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses are a feature of ulcerative col","E":"Distended foamy macrophages in the lamina propria indicate W"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has intermittent obstruction from ileal intussusception. What most likely started it?","choices":{"A":"A mass lead point","B":"Bowel wall edema","C":"Recent rotavirus","D":"Midgut rotation","E":"Thin stool water"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In older children and adults, intussusception is usually caused by an intraluminal mass or tumor acting as the traction point, and surgery is needed. Lymphoid hyperplasia from viral infection or rotavirus vaccination underlies intussusception in young children, whereas adult cases arise from a mass lead point. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intussusception). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intussusception causes intermittent pain, vomiting, and bloody currant-jelly stools (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception). COMLEX: up to 1% of patients with cystic fibrosis develop intussusception (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — segmentation versus peristalsis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Lymphoid hyperplasia from viral infection or rotavirus vacci"}},{"id":"grid_sdl29_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":4,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy who lives in a rural village with poor sanitation has chronic diarrhea, bulky greasy stools, and stunted growth, and he failed to respond to two doses of an oral vaccine. He has no family history of gastrointestinal disease and eats a mixed diet that includes wheat. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Fecal fat","Villus height","Anti-TG2 IgA","Serum apoB"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↓","↑","↔"],"B":["↑","↔","↔","↔"],"C":["↑","↔","↔","↓"],"D":["↑","↓","↔","↔"],"E":["↔","↔","↔","↔"]},"correct":"D","explanation":"Choice D (↑ fecal fat, ↓ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is correct. Environmental enteric dysfunction (tropical sprue) follows chronic exposure to fecal pathogens where sanitation is poor; the notes list stunting and high oral vaccine failure rates, and describe pronounced lamina propria immune cell infiltration with villous atrophy that is less advanced than in celiac disease. Loss of absorptive surface causes malabsorption with steatorrhea, and the disorder is non-genetic and not driven by gluten, so celiac serology is negative. Choice A (↑ fecal fat, ↓ villus height, ↑ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is celiac disease, in which IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is the most sensitive serum marker. Choice B (↑ fecal fat, ↔ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, as in cystic fibrosis, where intraluminal digestion fails but the mucosa is intact. Choice C (↑ fecal fat, ↔ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↓ serum apoB) is abetalipoproteinemia, in which absent apoB-containing lipoproteins leave enterocytes unable to export chylomicrons, causing steatorrhea in infancy with acanthocytes on the smear. Choice E (↔ fecal fat, ↔ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is lactase deficiency, an osmotic diarrhea from unabsorbed lactose without fat malabsorption. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 29, sections 4 and 5: general malabsorption, cystic fibrosis, celiac disease, EED, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia); Robbins Ch. 17 (malabsorption and diarrhea). The ↑ fecal fat for environmental enteric dysfunction is inferred from loss of absorptive surface, and intact villi in the pancreatic, abetalipoproteinemia, and lactase disorders are inferred because the sources describe no villous injury in them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: villous atrophy with negative celiac serology in a child from an area of poor sanitation points to environmental enteric dysfunction (Exam 2 notes, SDL 29). COMLEX: steatorrhea with normal villi suggests a luminal (pancreatic) or export (abetalipoproteinemia) defect rather than mucosal loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — micelle formation and chylomicron export","isHighYield":true}]};
